  ('The Fastest Ever 147 by Ronnie "The Rocket" O''Sullivan', 'the-fastest-ever-147-by-ronnie-the-rocket-osullivan', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Watch Ronnie "The Rocket" O''Sullivan hit the fastest ever maximum break (147) in only 5mins 20secs! That''s a perfect break, 15 reds with 15 backs followed by the yellow, green, brown, blue, pink and black in the time…', null, '"Ronnie picked up a cheque for 147,000 pounds (£235,200) in prize money for the maximum in the first round of the World Championships in 1997."', 'Watch Ronnie "The Rocket" O''Sullivan hit the fastest ever maximum break (147) in only 5mins 20secs! That''s a perfect break, 15 reds with 15 backs followed by the yellow, green, brown, blue, pink and black in the time it takes Ebdon to pick up his cue!

SIMPLY AMAZING

This has to be the best 147 break ever made and may never be beaten. Some people will just see a man potting some balls, others will see a real magician mastering his game in the way only he can. Ronnie picked up a cheque for 147,000 pounds ($235,200) in prize money for the maximum in the first round of the World Championships in 1997. He averaged just 8.8 seconds for each shot - Ronnie we salute you! A true snooker god!

WILL PROBABLY NEVER BE BEATEN?

St Blazey Snooker doesn''t think the speed of this break will ever be beaten, only Ronnie himself has come close to it in later years, we know Judd Trump has recorded a couple of fast 147''s but these have only been in practice. If you know of any 147''s being completed faster then please comment us below.', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('The Moore the Merrier', 'the-moore-the-merrier', 'League News', '[]'::jsonb, 'Congratulations go to Jake Moore from Bugle ‘B’ and his wife Michelle on the safe arrival of baby Kensa Mary last weekend – mum and baby are doing well.', null, '"The top game of the evening featured Tregonissey and St Blazey ‘B’, second and third respectively with the away side winning 3-2 in a night of surprisingly scrappy frames."', 'Congratulations go to Jake Moore from Bugle ‘B’ and his wife Michelle on the safe arrival of baby Kensa Mary last weekend – mum and baby are doing well.

more Jake popped into Bethel ‘A’ and helped his team to a great 4-1 victory in a night of four black-ball frames with Geoff Hawken’s victory over Ian Ball the only bright spot for the home side. The top game of the evening featured Tregonissey and St Blazey ‘B’, second and third respectively with the away side winning 3-2 in a night of surprisingly scrappy frames. St Blazey ‘A’ recorded the result of the night beating Lostwithiel ‘A’ 4-1 as messrs Dewings, Meares, Chris Brown with a 36 and Matty Woodward saw off Darren Lock in the final frame. Steve Court’s 32 break in the last frame was the deciding factor as St Neot returned from Lostwithiel ‘B’ with a 3-2 win whilst Bugle ‘A’ remain unbeaten as they walloped Mevagissey ‘A’ 4-1 with Mike Towsey’s single-point win over Mark Sturtridge the solitary point for the away side. Leaders of the Rees League Mevagisey ‘B’ suffered a 3-2 home defeat to Bethel ‘C’ who closed to within a point of their hosts thanks to victories by Crockford, Thomas and Smithson. Gorran Haven recorded their first victory with a stunning 4-1 win at Bugle ‘C’ – young Mike Pascoe the marksman. St Stephen continue to impress with a 4-1 win at Mevagissey ‘D’ with James Tomlinson stopping the whitewash by beating the previously unbeaten Anthony Harrison and Bethel ‘B’ moved menacingly into third place with a game in hand over the top two with a 5-0 home victory over Lerryn with Matt Green again starring with a 31 break.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bethel ‘A’ 1 (G Hawken) Bugle ‘B’ 4 (C Rundle, D Rundle, K Moore, J Moore), Bugle ‘A’ 4 (P Sturtridge, A Best, G Spencer (33), I Powell) Mevagissey ‘A’ 1 (M Towsey), Lostwithiel ‘B’ 2 (L Flower, R Broad) St Neot 3 (M Gregory, A Courts, S Courts (32)), St Blazey ‘A’ 4 (S Dewings, M Meares, C Brown (36), M Woodward) Lostwithiel ‘A’ 1 (D Courts), Tregonissey 2 (R Bilson, B McIntosh) St Blazey ‘B’ 3 (M Hancock, D Kirwan, P Baron). Rees Memorial League: Bugle ‘C’ 1 (M Pascoe) Gorran Haven 4 (R Bulley, A Reddall, P Grimmett, L McKinstry), Mevagissey ‘D’ 1 (J Tomlinson) St Stephen 4 (J Bennett, A Gardside, C Clark, B Green), Mevagissey ‘B’ 2 (N Shaw, B Rothwell) Bethel ‘C’ 3 (C Crockford, P Thomas, R Smithson), Bethel ‘B’ 5 (M Sweet, M Green (31), J Tamblyn, G Buckle, S Hicks) Lerryn 0. Top breaks of the week: G Spencer 33, S Courts 32, C Brown 36, M Green 31 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('The White Stuff', 'the-white-stuff', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Michael White held his nerve in a breath-taking finish to beat Xiao Guodong in the final of the Betway Shoot-Out in Blackpool.', null, null, 'Michael White held his nerve in a breath-taking finish to beat Xiao Guodong in the final of the Betway Shoot-Out in Blackpool.

more A sensational three days of fast and furious snooker at the Circus Arena came to a fitting conclusion in a final which went to the wire. China''s Xiao led 48-39 with 30 seconds left when he missed the penultimate red. Welshman White kept his cool to pot red, pink, red and black to take a decisive lead with just six seconds on the clock. The talented 23-year-old from Neath won the first professional title of his career and with it a bumper cheque for £32,000. A player known for his natural fluency, White beat Robert Milkins, Judd Trump, Tom Ford, Ben Woollaston and Kyren Wilson before his thrilling final victory. White''s snooker gift was recognised when, at the age of just nine, he became the youngest player ever to make a competitive century. At 14, he became the youngest winner of the world amateur title. In his first few years on the pro tour he struggled to make progress, but then made a breakthrough in 2012 by reaching the quarter-finals of the World Championship, beating boyhood idol Mark Williams in a memorable Crucible debut. Now into the world''s top 32 - the youngest player in that bracket - White certainly has the game, and the temperament, to become a prolific winner. He heads to Mumbai this weekend for the Indian Open hoping to carry his momentum into the world ranking event. Xiao -another of the sport''s rising stars who was runner-up at the Shanghai Masters last season - came agonisingly close to the title having impressed throughout the event with his calm determination at the table. The 26-year-old takes home £16,000. White said: "It''s been a brilliant tournament for me, I have played well all the way through. The final was unreal - I can''t wait to watch it back. I had won nothing before, so hopefully this will set me up for bigger things. "When I had the chance at the end it was a case of whether I could do it under pressure, so to compose myself and get over the line means everything to me. I could have missed any of the last four balls, even the last black. People don''t understand how nerve-racking it is. "But I felt calm all the way through the event, and just concentrated on my technique, keeping still and getting through the ball nicely. If you do that, you''ve got every chance." Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('The domination game', 'the-domination-game', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'In the 1970s there was Ray Reardon, in the 1980s there was Steve Davis and in the 1990s there was Stephen Hendry.', null, '"Stephen Hendry once won five consecutive ranking titles. This was extraordinary then but it would be even more so today because Hendry did not have as many players at the top of the game playing to the sort of standard he was capable of producing."', 'In the 1970s there was Ray Reardon, in the 1980s there was Steve Davis and in the 1990s there was Stephen Hendry.

more In seasons since, John Higgins, Ronnie O’Sullivan and Mark Williams have all had spells of dominance but will snooker ever again have a figure who wins the lion’s share of major titles over a prolonged period of time? Well, forever is a long time. But how about the next few years? Mark Selby is the world no.1 but he isn’t the game''s dominant player in the tradition of Reardon, Davis and Hendry. O’Sullivan is world champion but, although he dominates the headlines, he didn’t dominate last season. Nobody did. Several players had their moments. Most of the major titles were shared around. Why? Because there is a core group of around ten players who play snooker to a very high level and who are all capable of beating each other on the big occasions, and indeed they do so. There is very little between them, even though O’Sullivan is still regarded by many (including players) as better than the rest at his best. Stephen Hendry once won five consecutive ranking titles. This was extraordinary then but it would be even more so today because Hendry did not have as many players at the top of the game playing to the sort of standard he was capable of producing. Standards rise all the time in sport and snooker is no different. Through the ranks there are now more players able to play at a high standard. Often players play brilliantly and lose early in tournaments. But this is by no means the whole story. It isn’t just about ability but also mindset. The reason Davis and Hendry dominated, apart from how they played, was that they wanted to. They wanted to so much that they made the sacrifices necessary to dominate. Few players since have been as driven as that. Most come from humble, working class beginnings. When they start earning money in much greater amounts than they would have thought possible when young they become comfortable. They start to spend their money and enjoy themselves. This is entirely understandable. It is human. Many would feel it a sad state of affairs if they didn’t. But this approach doesn’t make champions who will threaten places in my fictional Mount Rushmore of snooker. The Davis’s and Hendry’s were the players who stopped in on Saturday night because they wanted to be up early on a Sunday morning to practise. They were the players who won a tournament and put it out of their minds. They were relentless in their belief that nothing was ever good enough, that success could always be bettered. Higgins and O’Sullivan have freely admitted they are not made this way, and Williams doesn’t seem to be either. If a player can earn £200,000 a year playing snooker, if they are happy to win a title or two a year, then they may well wonder why they should change. Even with the Davis/Hendry approach there is no guarantee a player could dominate again, such is the tough opposition out there. If it did happen and a player emerged who topped the rankings and won most of the major prizes, would it be a good thing for snooker? In some senses, yes. It would provide a focal point, as Phil Taylor has in darts, as the man to be shot at. The downside, though, is that many people do get bored watching the same player win everything, as if it takes away the sense of the unexpected. The question is, will it happen again? Well, if it does then it is going to take one very, very special player. Article courtesy of SnookerScene Images Courtesy of: Günter Leitenbauer & World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('The forgotten final', 'the-forgotten-final', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'We’re living in a golden age for snooker, but as with most previous golden ages most people won’t realise until it’s over.', null, '"A marker was laid down as to the standard in the opening frame, which O’Sullivan won with a 140 total clearance. He made another century, 126, and three half centuries to arrive at the interval leading 5-2. Hendry, for his part, had made a break of 101."', 'We’re living in a golden age for snooker, but as with most previous golden ages most people won’t realise until it’s over.

more The access to watching snooker tournaments now is greater than it’s ever been. You only have to go back a decade to a time where events outside the UK never made the TV back home and fans had to follow Teletext scores instead of instant live scoring. Yes, there really was a time before Eurosport’s blanket coverage, before live streaming, before Twitter, where people go to fulminate if they miss so much as a break-off shot, before blogs and forums and the endless chatter that surrounds modern sport. The more you give people, the more complacent they become. History, though, tells us how lucky we are. Let me take you back, way back, to a distant place known as 2003. You may have read about it. The 2002/03 season had begun in acrimony and rancour. So far, this does not distinguish it in any way from any other season. The players, egged on by their associates, rejected the Altium offer to bankroll the circuit and handed control instead to a couple of chancers on a ten-year contract. This contract would be torn up after less than a year due to their failure to deliver but it included a guarantee of eight ranking tournaments. With money tight (it tends to be when you turn it down) and tobacco sponsorship about to exit ashtray-right, putting on new events was going to be difficult. However, the WPBSA encouraged the organiser of the Irish Masters to turn his event into a ranking tournament and they then struck on the bright idea of staging the European Open not, as was traditional, on the continent but in a hotel in Torquay. Believe me, any Fawlty Towers references you can think of were trotted out with great regularity back then. The kicker was that there would be no TV coverage at all. There was to be no web streaming either. So the tournament unfolded in almost complete anonymity, which was a great shame because it produced one of the sport’s best finals that decade and with it one of Ronnie O’Sullivan’s best performances full stop. O’Sullivan was in a happy place generally at this point and had just got back into running, which gave him an outlet outside of snooker. He seemed relaxed in Torquay, perhaps because there were no demands from TV, and played some brilliant snooker to reach the final. And the final was a classic. His opponent was Stephen Hendry, who had just returned to form by winning the Welsh Open in fine style. A marker was laid down as to the standard in the opening frame, which O’Sullivan won with a 140 total clearance. He made another century, 126, and three half centuries to arrive at the interval leading 5-2. Hendry, for his part, had made a break of 101. He also made 88 in the first frame of the final session before O’Sullivan delivered another total clearance, a 142 total clearance. Hendry, always so dangerous in adversity, fought back as he so often had before, a 117 the highlight as he reduced his arrears to 6-5. But with two more big breaks, O’Sullivan emerged victorious at 9-6. And he was satisfied, not just to win the title but to do it so well against Hendry, an old foe and the player he most looked up to. Very few people watched this match. Two of them were Ray Reardon and Tony Knowles, who afterwards made comments to a local newspaper to the effect that there wasn’t enough safety play. When you can pot everything, safety isn’t quite as necessary. It was indicative of how snooker had changed, for the better most, if not Reardon and Knowles, would argue. Feeling good and with his game back in shape, O’Sullivan went on to win another excellent final, beating John Higgins 10-9 at the Irish Masters. What a shame hardly anyone saw what O’Sullivan still regards as one of his finest triumphs. It would be different today. One of the most significant of all Barry Hearn’s innovations could turn out to be liveworldsnooker.tv. It already shows PTCs and qualifiers exclusively and were there occasions where no broadcaster could be found for a future event, could be the place to watch it. It doesn’t hurt to remember that it wasn’t always like this. Article courtesy of SnookerScene Images Courtesy of: Action Images', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('The master cue man', 'the-master-cue-man', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'On the face of it there’s not much left to write about Steve Davis that hasn’t been said already. In 34 years on the circuit he has been one of snooker’s finest ambassadors, greatest winners and most relentlessly…', null, '“Barry Hearn actually challenged me to make it until I’m 60 and that is a great target to aim for. Who knows I could be the only player on the tour applying for a bus pass!"', 'On the face of it there’s not much left to write about Steve Davis that hasn’t been said already. In 34 years on the circuit he has been one of snooker’s finest ambassadors, greatest winners and most relentlessly disciplined professionals.

80''S DOMINATION

The remarkable thing is that, at 55, Davis still retains an almost childlike fascination with snooker. He has every right to have become thoroughly fed up with knocking balls around a table by now. But he hasn’t. You only have to speak to him for a few minutes to realise he remains one of snooker’s leading evangelists.

IF I WERE A BOXER?

Today he plays a 16 year-old, Zhu Yinghui, at the Shanghai Masters. Davis was one of the first professionals to play in China 30 years ago when he and his manager, Barry Hearn, explored new frontiers on and off the table. In Shanghai in 1999 I watched him miss a dolly blue to beat his old foe Stephen Hendry. He went on to lose 5-4. Davis disappeared after the match before turning up 20 minutes or so later for the press conference. “Sorry about that. I was upstairs in my room trying to kill myself,” he deadpanned. Chinese press, not accustomed to his sense of humour, looked at one another with some confusion, not to say alarm. In Beijing in 2005, at the China Open eventually won by Ding Junhui, Steve bashed his head on the thick steel door which formed the back entrance to the venue. I thought this was funny until I very quickly did the same thing. He was so dazed by it that he had to withdraw from his match with Ricky Walden after just a few frames. Only injury would ever cause Davis not to give it his all. He is held in respect by the other players because they see a man who has dedicated his life to his sport, who has achieved so much and who still wants to be part of it, still wants to play. For as long as there are balls to pot, Steve Davis will be trying his hardest. Article courtesy of the Snooker Scene Images Courtesy of: David Levene', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('The race is on for second spot', 'the-race-is-on-for-second-spot', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'With Tregonissey ‘A’ and Lostwithiel ‘B’ almost certain to win their leagues the race for runners-up spot is wide open in both leagues as the new play-off games start.', null, '"In the Rees League leaders Lostwithiel ‘B’ could have been crowned champions last night if Bethel ‘D’ don’t take at least three points at St Stephen ‘A’. They took all three points from their game against Lerryn, with Graham Hoskin and Gordon Brimicombe on target for the home side, but Josh Daniell, George Lory and Len Flower saw the away side home."', 'With Tregonissey ‘A’ and Lostwithiel ‘B’ almost certain to win their leagues the race for runners-up spot is wide open in both leagues as the new play-off games start.

more Mevagissey ‘A’ will be disappointed to drop three points at home to in-form Bugle ‘B’. Pete Chambers and Paul Williams got their points but Ian Ball, Mark Sturtridge and the resurgent Keith Moore took the points back to Bugle. Martin Powell’s excellent 43 break in his win over Andrew Gooch was Bethel ‘C’s only point at St Blazey ‘B’ which moved them into third place four points behind Mevagissey ‘A’. Paul Baron with a 34 followed by wins for Mike Hancock, Darren Kirwan and Graham Taylor saw Blazey home. Bethel ‘A’ just came out on top at home to St Blazey ‘A’ 3-2. Lee Rosevear and Stewart Dewings played well when winning for the away side but Geoff Hawken, Gary Stephens and Steve Benallack had victories for a very welcome home win. Bugle ‘A’ won at home 4-1 against Mevagissey ‘B’ to move into fourth place five points off the runners-up spot. Gary Spencer’s 48 and Andrew Best, with a 61 break, along with wins for Paul Sturtridge and Ian Powell saw them home. Dave Kendall scoring Meva’s point. In the Rees League leaders Lostwithiel ‘B’ could have been crowned champions last night if Bethel ‘D’ don’t take at least three points at St Stephen ‘A’. They took all three points from their game against Lerryn, with Graham Hoskin and Gordon Brimicombe on target for the home side, but Josh Daniell, George Lory and Len Flower saw the away side home. Steve Benallack’s demolition of Cary Clark in the final frame of the evening gained Bethel ‘D’ valuable breathing space chasing the runners-up spot. Anthony Garside and Dan Allen gained St Stephen ‘A’s points with Ryan Tonkin and Andy Spearman getting Bethel’s other points. Bethel ‘B’ look dangerous in fourth place after seeing off basement boys St Stephen ‘B’. Rob Garside had a walkover for the home side as Bethel were struck down by sickness but Dave Morshead, Clive Stuthridge, Rich Allen and Jim Tamblyn secured the away win. Mevagissey ‘D’ won their 12th game of the season beating Bugle ‘C’ 3-2. Richard Lakeman, Scott Mills and James Tomlinson got the home points with Garry Moore and Keith Moore getting the away points. Brother Ben and Charlie Prynn along with Joe Chesterfield saw Mevagissey ‘C’ return from Gorran Haven with a 3-2 win with Paul Grimmett and Lee McKinstry registering for the home side.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bethel ‘A’ 3 (G Hawken, G Stephens, S Benallack) St Blazey ‘A’ 2 (L Roseveare, S Dewings), Mevagissey ‘A’ 2 (P Chambers, P Williams) Bugle ‘B’ 3 (I Ball, M Sturtridge, K Moore), St Blazey ‘B’ 4 (P Baron (34), M Hancock, D Kirwan, G Taylor) Bethel ‘C’ 1 (M Powell (43)), Bugle ‘A’ 4 (G Spencer (48), P Sturtridge, A Best (61), I Powell) Mevagissey ‘B’ 1 (D Kendall). Rees Memorial League: Bethel ‘D’ 3 (R Tonkin, A Spearman, S Benallack) St Stephen ‘A’ 2 (A Garside, D Allen), Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (R Lakeman, S Mills, J Tomlinson) Bugle ‘C’ 2 (G Moore, K Moore), Lerryn 2 (G Hoskin, G Brimicombe) Lostwithiel ‘B’ 3 (J Daniell, G Lory, L Flower), St Stephen ‘B’ 1 (R Garside) Bethel ‘B’ 4 (D Morshead, C Stuthridge, R Allen, J Tamblyn), Gorran Haven 2 (P Grimmett, L McKinstry) Mevagissey ‘C’ 3 (B Prynn, J Chesterfield, C Prynn). Top breaks of the week: P Baron 34, M Powell 43, G Spencer 48, A Best 61 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('The return of Parrott', 'the-return-of-parrott', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'The Crucible Theatre is associated with nerves and tension but John Parrott demonstrated a rare confidence when he won the world title in 1991.', null, '"Parrott had exorcised the Davis demons in the semi-finals with a 16-10 defeat of the 1980s Crucible king and played with remarkable fluency as the final began, a century and four half centuries to blow White away."', 'The Crucible Theatre is associated with nerves and tension but John Parrott demonstrated a rare confidence when he won the world title in 1991.

more “When I walked in the building on the first day I just fancied winning it,” he said years later in recalling the greatest triumph of his career. Parrott certainly looked confident in the final, winning all seven frames of the first session against Jimmy White. He nursed this lead all the way to an 18-11 victory. It was the fulfilment of a potential obvious since Parrott’s teenage days, when he appeared on the BBC’s Junior Pot Black sporting a chocolate brown suit which, for some reason, never caught on. Anyone who has seen Parrott providing punditry for the BBC know him as a chirpy scouser with a ready wit but this belies his competitiveness. He was hard as nails. He qualified for the Crucible initially in his debut season, 1983/84, and made an immediate impact by beating Tony Knowles, the fourth seed, 10-7. He ran Dennis Taylor close in round two, losing only 13-11, and the following year took Ray Reardon the distance in the quarter-finals before losing 13-12. By 1988, Parrott was emerging as one of the favourites. He had appeared in his first ranking final, the Mercantile Classic, and being in his 20s was well placed to take advantage of the decline of the ageing legends who still dominated. Parrott’s career in the top flight straddled two eras: those of Steve Davis and Stephen Hendry. The three of them plus Jimmy White represented an authentic big four, players who played the modern way and who each inspired the other. In 1989, Parrott reached the final but by the time it arrived had nothing left to give. The tournament had begun in the shadow of the Hillsborough disaster a few miles from the Crucible. Parrott was an Everton fan but had spent many afternoons at Anfield. For his first round match against Steve James he wore a black armband as a mark of respect for the dead. The emotion had contributed to the sapping of his mental reserves and it didn’t help that he faced Davis in the final, the winner for the last two years and five times in total. Privately, Davis was unhappy with his game but this was not reflected in his performance. He inflicted on Parrott a humiliation: an 18-3 defeat with a session to spare. It remains the greatest margin of victory in any Crucible match. Ludicrously, Parrott was then made to play an exhibition against Davis in the final free evening session. Spirits were, to put it mildly, decidedly low. Hendry beat him in the semi-finals in 1990 but the Scot’s title defence ended in the quarter-finals in 1991 and Parrott would face White, under pressure to win the sport’s biggest prize, in the final. Parrott had exorcised the Davis demons in the semi-finals with a 16-10 defeat of the 1980s Crucible king and played with remarkable fluency as the final began, a century and four half centuries to blow White away. He led 11-5 going into the final day of the championship but was still nervous. “I was never going to get any sleep. I’d have had better luck nailing a blancmange to the ceiling,” he said afterwards. Parrott’s title defence began promisingly in 1992 when he beat Eddie Charlton 10-0 in what remains the Crucible’s only whitewash but he eventually lost 13-12 to Alan McManus in the quarter-finals, a stage he would reach six more times without going any further. Parrott played at the Crucible every year until 2004 and again in 2006 and 2007, where he edged Davis 10-9 and held Shaun Murphy to 8-8 before losing the last five frames. Tomorrow, Parrott gets the 2012 World Championship underway when the qualifiers begin in Sheffield. He retired from competitive play last year but, as a WPBSA member, is entitled to enter the game’s blue riband event. He has drawn Patrick Wallace, one of the best players in this section, and is presumably not expecting much, but such is the allure of the World Championship, such are the golden memories he, like so many, have of the tournament, that he couldn’t resist. Article courtesy of SnookerScene Images Courtesy of: Action Images', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Thepchaiya reaches nooh level', 'thepchaiya-reaches-nooh-level', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Thailand’s Thepchaiya Un-Nooh won his first professional title by beating Liang Wenbo 8-2 in the final of the SangSom Six Red World Championship.', null, null, 'Thailand’s Thepchaiya Un-Nooh won his first professional title by beating Liang Wenbo 8-2 in the final of the SangSom Six Red World Championship.

more World number 47 Un-Nooh delighted his fans in Bangkok by dominating the final against China’s Liang, winning seven frames in a row from 2-1 down. His top break was 55 as he cruised to his first title on the World Snooker Tour and a prize of 2.5 million Baht, around £45,000. Un-Nooh, age 30, established himself as one to watch in 2008 when he won the world amateur title. He turned pro in 2009, but struggled to fulfill his potential in his first few years on tour. Last season was the best of his career as he reached the semi-finals of the Indian Open ranking event and the final of the Xuzhou Open Asian Tour event, climbing into the world’s top 50 for the first time. And now he has secured his first trophy, having come through the group phase before knocking out Stuart Bingham, Judd Trump, Boonyarit Kaettikun, Noppon Saenkgham and world number 21 Liang. Un-Nooh’s victory could also earn him a place at the Champion of Champions event in Coventry in November. Article & Images courtesy of the World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('There''s a Lock in for Lostwithiel', 'theres-a-lock-in-for-lostwithiel', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'There was a remarkable night at Bethel as Lostwithiel ‘A’ trounced Bethel ‘D’ 3-1 in the team pairs.', null, '"St Neot picked up a very useful 4-1 home victory over in-form Bugle ‘B’ who had Keith More to thank for their solitary frame and Bryan Warne picked up his and Lostwithiel’s only point as Bethel ‘A’ left it late into the night to complete a much-needed win."', 'There was a remarkable night at Bethel as Lostwithiel ‘A’ trounced Bethel ‘D’ 3-1 in the team pairs.

more There was a remarkable night at Bethel as Lostwithiel ‘A’ trounced Bethel ‘D’ 3-1 in the team pairs. Bethel were so far behind they forfeited the last two frames mainly due to Darren Lock’s superb 95 and 53 breaks. Lock then produced a 32 in the league match but Andy Spearman, Jason Pascoe and Richie Allen upset the form book for the Bethel boys to register their first league win. St Blazey ‘B’ closed the gap on Bugle ‘A’ to just two points with a game in hand by whitewashing Mevagissey ‘A’ as Bugle dropped two points at home to Tregonissey as Chris Stephenson and Barry ‘back in the fold’ McIntosh with a 32, pegged back the table toppers. St Neot picked up a very useful 4-1 home victory over in-form Bugle ‘B’ who had Keith More to thank for their solitary frame and Bryan Warne picked up his and Lostwithiel’s only point as Bethel ‘A’ left it late into the night to complete a much-needed win. Top match of the night in the Rees saw Mevagissey ‘C’ visit Bethel ‘C’ who produced some solid snooker to cement their promotion challenge and regain top spot in the league with a 4-1 victory with Ben Prynne on the mark for seasiders. Bethel ‘B’ slipped up at Mevagissey ‘D’ as Mike Byard’s blackball win over Guy Buckle clinched a 3-2 win for the home side. Veteran Mo Roscorla stopped Sparks from whitewashing Bugle ‘C’ as he overcame Bert Bampton in the last frame but Sparks have moved menacingly into the promotion scalp. And St Stephen suffered a surprise 3-2 home defeat to Lerryn as Graham Hoskin, Terry Middleton and Charles McMiram took the points for the visitors who are in a rich vein of form at present.

TEAM PAIRS

The team pairs draw has been finalised with the games to be played on Tuesday, December 17: Bethel ‘A’ v Tregonissey, Lostwithiel ‘A’ v St Blazey ‘B’, Lostwithiel ‘B’ v Bugle ‘A’, Mevagissey ‘B’ v St Blazey ‘A’.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bethel ‘D’ 3 (J Pascoe, R Allen, A Spearman) Lostwithiel ‘A’ 2 (C Perring, D Lock (32)), Bugle ‘A’ 3 (P Sturtridge, A Peers, I Powell) Tregonissey 2 (C Stephenson, B McIntosh (32)), Lostwithiel ‘B’ 1 (B Warne) Bethel ‘A’ 4 (B Stephens, G Hawken, L Collard, G Stephens), Mevagissey ‘A’ 0 St Blazey ‘B’ 5 (D Kirwan (33), M Hancock, P Baron, G Taylor, A Gooch), St Neot 4 (A Tamblyn, S Courts, M Pearce, A Courts) Bugle ‘B’ 1 (K Moore). Rees Memorial League: Bugle ‘C’ 1 (M Roscorla) Sparks 4 (M Williams, R Bolt, C Stuthridge, M Bolt), Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (R Blamey, J Tomlinson, M Byard) Bethel ‘B’ 2 (Matt Green, S Hicks), St Stephen 2 (A Garside, J Rosevear) Lerryn 3 (G Hoskin, T Middleton, C McMiram), Bethel ‘C’ 4 (C Crockford, R Smithson, S Jones, H Brett) Mevagissey ‘C’ 1 (B Prynn). Top breaks of the week: D Lock 32, B McIntosh 32 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Three-way lead in Victory League', 'three-way-lead-in-victory-league', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tywardreath ‘A’ picked up another good win in the St Blazey and District Snooker League beating Bethel ‘A’ 4-1.', null, '"Gary Spencer had a ball in the Bugle derby with a 43-break in the ‘A’ side’s 4-1 win."', 'Tywardreath ‘A’ picked up another good win in the St Blazey and District Snooker League beating Bethel ‘A’ 4-1.

more Martin Powell picked up Bethel’s solitary point with an amazing 84-73 win over Alan Dennis. Tregonissey bounced back after last week’s loss by beating Bodmin-based Sparks 4-1. Gary Spencer had a ball in the Bugle derby with a 43-break in the ‘A’ side’s 4-1 win. Lostwithiel SC ‘B’ recorded a fine 3-2 victory over Mevagissey ‘A’ – George Lory with a thrilling re-spotted black win over Leighton Varcoe. St Blazey meanwhile had another 5-0 win; this time at Lerryn to open up an early lead in the Rees League.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Mevagissey ‘B’ 1 (B Rothwell) Lostwithiel SC ‘A’ (R Broad, A Maddams, D Lock, C Dickinson); Lostwithiel SC ‘B’ 3 (L Flowers, G Lory, J Daniell) Mevagissey ‘A’ 2 (P Staley, M Fox); Tywardreath ‘A’ 4 (A Gooch, P Barron, D Kirwan, B Phillips) Bethel ‘A’ 1 (M Powell); Sparks 1 (S Bampton) Tregonissey 4 (R Bilson, C Kendall, R Pearson, B McIntosh); Bugle ‘A’ 4 (G Spencer, I Powell, P Strutridge, A Best) Bugle ‘B’ 1 (G Taylor). Rees Memorial League: Lerryn 0 St Blazey 5 (L Reynolds, L Roseveare, M Mears, M Woodward, C Brown); Bethel ‘B’ 3 (G Buckle, M Green, S Hicks) Bethel ‘C’ 2 (J Cocks, H Brett); Bethel ‘D’ 4 (S Bennallack, T Stephen, L Collard, J Pascoe) Tywardreath ‘B’ 1 (P Hunt); Gorran Haven 2 (R Holmes, L McKinstry) Bethel ‘C’ 3 (K Spencer, K Moore, J Moore). Top breaks of the week: Gary Spencer 43, Andrew Gooch 33. Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks

WEEK 2 IN PICTURES

