  ('Tight at the top of the Rees League', 'tight-at-the-top-of-the-rees-league', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'It’s still neck and neck at the top of the Rees League as both Bethel ‘C’ and Mevagissey ‘C’ gained hard-earned 3-2 wins.', null, 'James Popplewell’s 31 and Matt Mills’s 32 turned things aroung only for Phil Hooley to level things up beating Leighton Varcoe, with Paul Williams getting the better of Andrew Gooch in the decider for the away win.', 'It’s still neck and neck at the top of the Rees League as both Bethel ‘C’ and Mevagissey ‘C’ gained hard-earned 3-2 wins.

more Bethel won two respot blacks and also won a frame on the black to sneak past an unlucky Lerryn side. Howard Brett and Courtney Crockford along with Ben Stunnell got their wins with Matt Woodward and Les Shakespeare replying. The Meva boys were thankful for Adrian Evans’ final frame respot black win over Guy Buckle to keep them level at the top. Clive Stuthridge and Jim Tamblyn got the home points with Ben Pryn and Matt Chesterfield levelling up to set up the exciting final frame. Bugle ‘C’ got a very good win at St Stephen and with games in hand will surely climb the table. Chris Brown and Dan Parmenter’s black-ball victory over Gary Moore was as good as it got for the home side with Damian Knight, Mo Roscorla and Roy Bayliss securing the win. Gorran took four points at Tregonissey ‘C’ with Steve Williams, Andy Redall, Rob Bunney and Lee McKinstry their winners with Graham Rosevear beating Geoff Hayhurst in the opener. Tregonissey ‘A’ picked up a good 3-2 win at Bethel ‘A’ and lead the table by seven points but Richard Pearson and Barrie McIntosh put them 2-0 up but Matt Green and Garry Stephens’ best performance of the season levelled things up with Ryan Bilson’s excellent tactical frame win over Leon Collard sealing the win. Bugle ‘A’ won 4-1 at Mevagissey ‘D’ to close the gap on the leaders by a point. Andrew Best, Jake Moore, Ian Ball and Gary Spencer got their points with Mark Ashton beating Paul Sturtridge for the home point. Steve Courts with a 30 and Darren Lock’s 49 break got St Neot off to a flier at Bugle ‘B’. Andy Ridgeman got their third point beating Mark Sturtridge with Keith Moore and Colin Rundle beating Matt Vessey and Colin Rundle beating Matt Vessey and Anthony Maddams respectivley for the home side. Mike Hooley’s victory over Phil Staley got Tregonissey ‘C’ ahead at home to Mevagissey ‘A’. James Popplewell’s 31 and Matt Mills’s 32 turned things aroung only for Phil Hooley to level things up beating Leighton Varcoe, with Paul Williams getting the better of Andrew Gooch in the decider for the away win. Mevagissey ‘B’ got three precious points at home to St Blazey to jump above their ‘D’ team in the table. Matt Chesterfield, Matt Rowe and Ben Rothwell got the win with Stewart Dewings and Chris Morford on target for the visitors.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Tregonissey ‘B’ 2 (M Hooley, P Hooley) Mevagissey ‘A’ 3 (J Popplewell (31), M Mills (32), P Williams), Mevagissey ‘D’ 1 (M Ashton) Bugle ‘A’ 4 (A Best, J Moore, G Spencer, I Ball), Bethel ‘A’ 2 (M Green, G Stephens) Tregonissey ‘A’ 3 (R Pearson, B McIntosh, R Bilson), Mevagissey ‘B’ 3 (M Chesterfield, M Rowe, B Rothwell) St Blazey 2 (S Dewings, C Morford), Bugle ‘B’ 2 (K Moore, C Rundle) St Neot 3 (S Courts (30), D Lock (49), A Ridgeman). Rees Memorial League: Tregonissey ‘C’ 1 (G Rosevear) Gorran 4 (S Williams, A Reddall, R Bunney, L McKinstry), St Stephen 2 (C Brown, D Parmenter) Bugle ‘C’ 3 (D Knight, M Roscorla, R Bayliss), Bethel ‘B’ 2 (C Stuthridge, J Tamblyn) Mevagissey ‘C’ 3 (B Prynn, M Chesterfield, A Evans), Lerryn 2 (M Woodward, L Shakespear) Bethel ‘C’ 3 (H Brett, C Crockford, B Stunnell). Top breaks of the week: J Popplewell 31, M Mills 32, S Courts 30, D Lock 49. Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Ton-up for Chambers in impressive 115 clearance', 'ton-up-for-chambers-in-impressive-115-clearance', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'A magnificent season’s top break of 115 (clearance) by Pete Chambers of Mevagissey ‘A’ has helped them open up a five-point lead at the top of the Victory League', null, 'St Stephen ‘A’ pipped Bethel ‘B’ 3-2 to stay in contention. John Bennett’s single-point victory over Dave Morshead got them going and Anthony Garside got their second point against Jim Tamblyn but it took a black ball victory for Dan Allen over Rich Allen to complete the win and also leaves Dan still unbeaten.', 'A magnificent season’s top break of 115 (clearance) by Pete Chambers of Mevagissey ‘A’ has helped them open up a five-point lead at the top of the Victory League

more Pete’s break against Anthony Maddams will take some beating and with Matt Mills weighing in with a 33 along with wins for Paul Williams and Chris Kendall it was a great night’s work for the Meva boys. With only Phil Staley failing in the final frame against Darren Lock. There were six breaks of 30 or more in the St Blazey derby. Nick Kendall started it all off having a 44 when beating Paul Baron. Mickey Meares had a 30 but lost to Graham Taylor, who popped in a fine 49. Andrew Gooch then weighed in with 50 followed by Mike Hancock’s 41 and 39 when playing Stewart Dewings in the final frame. Leigh Rosevear beat Dave Grose to claim the ‘A’ team’s second point. Ian Ball was Bugle ‘A’s only success at home to Tregonissey ‘A’ which is a big blow to their title aspirations. But gives Tregonissey a real boost as they moved to equal second. Ryan Bilson produced excellent 55 and 38 breaks when beating Ian Powell. There were also wins for Barrie McIntosh, Jason Simmons and Jason Cocks with Ian Ball getting the home team’s point. Bethel ‘D’ really needed more than the two points they got at home to Bugle ’N’ as they needed to try and pull their opponents back to the relegation spots. Ryan Tonkin and Jason Pascoe traded frames with Colin Rundle and Mark Sturtridge only for Dave Rundle to beat Steve Benallack on a respotted black. Bethel ‘C’ looked doomed after a 4-1 defeat to their ‘A’ side. Martin Powell played well to see off Gary Stephens but wins for Matt Green, Andy Peers, Bob Stephens and Leon Collard saw their opponents home. Bodmin Q-Bar pushed leaders Mevagissey ‘B’ all the way but lost 3-2 with Richie Bolt and Steve Bampton winners, whilst Malcolm Meneer and Brian Barnicoat pushed Mark Gregory and Ben Rothwell all the way. St Stephen ‘A’ pipped Bethel ‘B’ 3-2 to stay in contention. John Bennett’s single-point victory over Dave Morshead got them going and Anthony Garside got their second point against Jim Tamblyn but it took a black ball victory for Dan Allen over Rich Allen to complete the win and also leaves Dan still unbeaten. St Stephen ‘B’ got a 5-0 thrashing at Trgeonissey ‘B’ despite three very close frames. Gary Wherry (Craig Nicholls) and Michael Hooley (Terry Nicholls) sneaked past their opponents followed by wins for Chris Robins, Justin and Phil Hooley. Gorran returned from Lerryn with a 3-2 victory. Terry Middleton kicked off the home team’s night beating Richard Holmes but Lee McKinstry and Rob Bunney replied. Graham Hoskin levelled things up but Andy Reddall got past McMiram in the decider.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bugle ‘A’ 1 (Ian Ball) Tregonissey ‘A’ 4 (Barrie McIntosh, Jason Simmons, Jason Cocks, Ryan Bilson (55+38), St Blazey ‘B’ 3 (Graham Taylor (49), Andrew Gooch (50), Mike Hancock (41+39) St Blazey ‘A’ 2 (Nick Kendall (44) – Mickey Meares (30 but lost), Mevagissey ‘A’ 4 (Matt Mills (44), Paul Williams, Pete Chambers (115 clearance), Chris Kendall) St Neot 1 (Darren Lock), Bethel ‘C’ 1 (Martin Powell) Bethel ‘A’ 4 (Matt Green, Andy Peers, Bob Stephens, Leon Collard), Bethel ‘D’ 2 (Ryan Tonkin, Jason Pascoe) Bugle ‘B’ 3 (Colin Rundle, Mark Sturtridge, Dave Rundle). Rees Memorial League: St Stephen ‘A’ 3 (John Bennett, Anthony Garside, Dan Allen) Bethel ‘B’ 2 (Guy Buckle, Clive Stuthridge), Lerryn 2 (Terry Middleton, Graham Hoskin) Gorran Haven 3 (Lee McKinstry, Rob Bunney, Andy Reddall), Tregonissey ‘B’ 5 (Gary Wherry, Chris Robins, Justin Hooley, Michael Hooley, Philip Hooley) St Stephen ‘B’ 0, Bodmin Q-Bar 2 (Richard Bolt, Steve Bampton) Mevagissey ‘B’ 3 (Nathan Shaw, Ben Rothwell, Mark Gregory). Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: P Chambers 115, M Mills 33, N Kendall 44, M Mears 30, A Gooch 50, M Hancock 41 & 39 Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Top three claim the spoils with 3-2 victories', 'top-three-claim-the-spoils-with-3-2-victories', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Its as you were at the top of the St Blazey and District Victory League as all the top three won 3-2 but fourth-placed Tregonissey ‘A’ closed the gap with a 4-1 win.', null, 'In the Rees League a new high break of 38 was set by Matt Rowe of Mevagissey ‘B’ as the leaders had a very close 3-2 win over their ‘D’ team. Joe Chesterfield and Ben Prynn both lost out on the black in trying to add to Matt Chesterfield’s victory Mark Gregory as the leaders stay nine points clear at the top.', 'Its as you were at the top of the St Blazey and District Victory League as all the top three won 3-2 but fourth-placed Tregonissey ‘A’ closed the gap with a 4-1 win.

more Leaders Mevagissey ‘A’ were thankful for Matt Mills’ black ball win over Leon Collard for their victory. Earlier Andy Peers and Gary Stephens played excellently to see Phil Staley and Chris Kendall but Pete Chambers and Paul Williams, who had a 42 break, along with Mills saw the table-toppers home. Bugle ‘A’ had a nervy odd-point win at St Blazey ‘A’. Nick Kendall inflicted a second defeat of the season on Gary Spencer to give the home side the perfect start but wins for Paul Sturtridge, Ian Ball, who sneaked past Stewart Dewings, and Andrew Best put them 3-1 up only for Leigh Rosevear to get the better of Ian Powell in the final frame. St Blazey ‘B’ dropped two points at Bugle ‘B’. Dave Rundle’s single point win over Paul Baron followed in the final frame with Mark Sturtridge’s respot win over a well-oiled Dave Grose gave Bugle their two points with Graham Taylor, Jamie Read and Mike Hancock who made a 59 were Blazey’s winners. St Neot completed a second 4-1 victory this season over Pelynt. Richard Broad’s excellent victory over Cyril Dickenson was the away sides only bright spot as Chris Perring, Anthony Maddams, Darren Lock and Steve Courts completed the job for the home side. Barrie McIntosh returned to the Treggy fold in the absence of Ryan Bilson as they beat Bethel ‘D’ 4-1. Ryan Tonkin continued his own excellent form when beating Jason Cocks for Bethel’s point. But McIntosh, Martin Knight, Jason Simmons and Chris Stephenson helped Treggy close the gap on the leaders. In the Rees League a new high break of 38 was set by Matt Rowe of Mevagissey ‘B’ as the leaders had a very close 3-2 win over their ‘D’ team. Joe Chesterfield and Ben Prynn both lost out on the black in trying to add to Matt Chesterfield’s victory Mark Gregory as the leaders stay nine points clear at the top. Bodmin Q-Bar moved back into second place courtesy of a 3-2 win at Gorran Haven. Paul Grimmett opened the home side’s account seeing off Malc Meneer but wins for Richie Bolt and Phil Ashton sandwiched between Rob Bunney’s victory over Brian Barnicoat left a final frame shoot-out between Lee McKinstry and Steve Bampton which Bamps took 51-32. Graham Hoskin was Lerryn’s only bright spot at Mevagissey ‘D’ but Dave Vipond was unlikely to lose out on the black to Rob Blamey. George Lory, Richard Lakeman, 30 break, and Neil Parker secured the other home points. Tregonissey ‘B’ felt hard done by at Bethel ‘B’ losing 4-1 but could of sneaked a couple more frames with Ryan Thomas and Michael Hooley pushing Guy Buckle and Rich Allen respectively. Philip Hooley was Treggy’s only winner as Bethel closed to win two points of St Stephen ‘A’.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: St Blazey ‘A’ 2 (Nick Kendall, Leigh Rosevear) Bugle ‘A’ 3 (Paul Sturtridge, Ian Ball, Andrew Best), Tregonissey ‘A’ 4 (Martin Knight, Barrie McIntosh, Jason Simmons, Chris Stephenson) Bethel ‘D 1 (Ryan Tonkin), Bethel ‘A’ 2 (Andy Peers, Gary Stephens) Mevagissey ‘A’ 3 (Pete Chambers, Paul Williams (42), Matt Mills), St Neot 4 (Chris Perring, Anthony Maddams, Darren Lock, Steve Courts) Pelynt 1 (Richard Broad), Bugle ‘B’ 2 (Dave Rundle, Mark Sturtridge) St Blazey ‘B’ 3 (Graham Taylor, Jamie Read, Mike Hancock (59). Rees Memorial League: Bethel ‘B’ 4 (Jim Tamblyn, Guy Buckle, Clive Stuthridge, Rich Allen) Tregonissey ‘B’ 1 (Phil Hooley), Mevagissey ‘D’ 4 (George Lory, Rich Lakeman (30), Rob Blamey, Neil Parker) Lerryn 1 (Graham Hoskin), Gorran Haven 2 (Paul Grimmett, Rob Bunney) Bodmin Pool 3 (Richard Bolt, Phil Ashton, Steve Bampton), Mevagissey ‘B’ 4 (Matt Rowe (38), Dave Kendall, Nathan Shaw, Ben Rothwell) Mevagissey ‘C’ 1 (Matt Chesterfield). Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: P Williams 42, M Hancock 59, M Rowe 38 Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Top two drop points as title race hots up', 'top-two-drop-points-as-title-race-hots-up', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'It’s as you were at the top of the St Blazey and District Snooker League Victory League as both St Blazey ‘B’ and Bugle ‘A’ dropped two points apiece in their latest matches.', null, '"In the Rees League Lerryn are enjoying a much improved season and Gordon Brimicombe, Terry Middleton and league stalwart Graham Hoskin saw them home against Mevagissey ‘C’ – whose season seems to be slipping away."', 'It’s as you were at the top of the St Blazey and District Snooker League Victory League as both St Blazey ‘B’ and Bugle ‘A’ dropped two points apiece in their latest matches.

more Breaks of 36 by Baz Baron and 30 by Andy Gooch along with a Mike Hancock victory saw St Blazey ‘B’ through against their ‘A’ side and despite a 37-break by Darren Kirwan his opponent Matt Woodward took the frame on the black, while Chris Brown scored a good victory over Graham Taylor. Bugle ‘A’ meanwhile raced into a 3-0 lead at Loswithiel ‘B’ but victories for Richard Broad over Andy Best and John Daniell amassing 87 points when defeating Gary Spencer may leave the Bugle boys rueing their dropped points in the title run-in. Tregonissey consolidated third place with a hard-fought 3-2 win at Bethel ‘A’ with Gary Stephen’s 30 and Ryan Bilson’s 35 the highlights – Bethel undone by losing two black-ball frames. Bugle ‘B’ jumped up the table after despatching Lostwithiel ‘A’ 4-1, Darren Lock getting the away side’s point after seeing off Arsen Moore. St Neot enjoyed a 4-1 home win over basement boys Bethel ‘D’ with Ryan Tonkin’s great victory over Steve Courts the standout frame of the evening. In the Rees League Lerryn are enjoying a much improved season and Gordon Brimicombe, Terry Middleton and league stalwart Graham Hoskin saw them home against Mevagissey ‘C’ – whose season seems to be slipping away. Mevagissey ‘D’ still fancy a late run for the title with a much more settled side they overcame Bugle ‘C’ 3-2 and are 10 points off the top, but with a game in hand. Three very tight frames all went the way of Bethel ‘B’ over Bodmin Sparks to help them to a 5-0 whitewash to move to within a point of the top two. Previous leaders Bethel ‘C’ went down to a rare defeat at St Stephen as Anthony Garside, John Bennett and Cary Clark recorded victories for the Clay Country side, who are themselves only four points off the top. Bethel have been joined on 37 points by Mevagissey ‘B’ who had a hard-fought 3-2 victory over Gorran Haven. Richard Holmes and Rob Bunney were on target for the Haven by Dave Kendall convincingly posted a victory in the final frame to take the points back to Meva.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: St Neot 4 (I Wilton, M Courts, T Farrant, A Courts) Bethel ‘D’ 1 (R Tonkin (43)), Lostwithiel ‘B’ 2 (R Broad, J Daniell) Bugle ‘A’ 3 (A Peers, P Sturtridge, I Powell), Bugle ‘B’ 4 (D Rundle, M Sturtridge, J Moore, I Ball), Lostwithiel ‘A’ 1 (D Lock), St Blazey ‘B’ 3 (P Baron (36), M Hancock, A Gooch (30) – lost D Kirwan (37) St Blazey ‘A’ 2 (C Brown, M Woodward), Bethel ‘A’ 2 (M Green, G Stephens (30), Tregonissey 3 (C Stephenson, M Fox, R Bilson (34). Rees Memorial League: Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (J Tomlinson, R Lakeman, S Mills) Bugle ‘C’ 2 (J Conroy, K Spencer), Gorran Haven 2 (R Holmes, R Bunney) Mevagissey ‘B’ 3 (M Gregory, B Rothwell, D Kendall), Bethel ‘B’ 5 (M Green, G Buckle, S Hicks, J Tamblyn, M Sweet) Sparks 0, Lerryn 3 (G Hoskin, G Brimicombe, T Middleton) Mevagissey ‘C’ 2 (C Prynn, M Chesterfield), St Stephen 3 (A Gardside, J Bennett, C Clark) Bethel ‘C’ 2 (H Brett, R Smithson). Top breaks of the week: R Tonkin 43, P Baron 36, A Gooch 30, D Kirwan 37 G Stephens 30, R Bilson 34 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Treggy clinch the Victory League title', 'treggy-clinch-the-victory-league-title', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'With only one week of the season left all issues bar one have been settled with only who gets relegated to the Rees League to be settled.', null, '"Mevagissey ‘D’s strong finish to the season continues as they beat their ‘C’ team 4-1. They were surely disappointed with their early season form but will look to correct that next season to mount a serious title challenge. On the night Rob Blamey, Scott Mills, James Tomlinson and Rich Lakeman were winners with Matt Chesterfield replying."', 'With only one week of the season left all issues bar one have been settled with only who gets relegated to the Rees League to be settled.

more Tregonissey ‘A’ claimed the title with a 5-0 whitewash of Lostwithiel ‘A’. Ryan Bilson''s 34 and Jason Cocks’ victory over Darren Lock were the highlights of the evening as Treggy deservedly claimed the silverware as they led the league from October onwards. Mevagissey ‘A’ were confirmed as runners-up as a result of the St Blazey ‘B’ 3 Bugle ‘A’ 2 result, they now cannot be caught going into last night’s final games. Paul Baron’s 54, Mike Hancock’s 36 and a win for Andrew Gooch were good enough for their victory with Andrew Best and Gary Spencer replying. Bethel ‘C’ came from 2-0 down against their ‘A’ team as they battle to stay up as only one team is relegated this season. Leon Collard and Geoff Hawken (36) put the ‘A’ firmly in control but Howard Brett (30), Martin Powell and Paul Thomas, who obliterated Gary Stephens in the final frame, sealed an excellent win. That result severely dented Meva ‘B’s chances of survival as they crashed to a 4-1 home loss to St Blazey ‘A’. Only Mark Gregory could win leaving them with a massive task on their hands last night at Bethel ‘A’. In the Rees League champions Lostwithiel ‘A’ had a walkover at St Stephen ‘A’ – who were unable to raise a side. Runners-up spot was claimed by Bethel ‘D’ as they beat Tregonissey ‘B’ 4-1. Ryan Tonkin (31) along with Steve Benallack, Andy Spearman and Jason Pascoe got their win with Chris Robins seeing off Ben Stunnell in reply. Mevagissey ‘D’s strong finish to the season continues as they beat their ‘C’ team 4-1. They were surely disappointed with their early season form but will look to correct that next season to mount a serious title challenge. On the night Rob Blamey, Scott Mills, James Tomlinson and Rich Lakeman were winners with Matt Chesterfield replying. Gorran were 2-0 down at home to basement boys St Stephen ‘B’ through Robert Garside and Craig Nicholls but rallied to win with victories for Rob Bunney, Richard Holmes and Paul Grimmett. The first league finals took place last week as Barrie McIntosh and Darren Lock played in the singles final at Lerryn. Darren went two frames up and looked favourite to win but Barrie fought back to take the next four frames for victory. There were six breaks on the night. The following evening the Seniors title was at St Blazey between Geoff Hawken and Len Flower. After an even first frame which Len nicked he deservedly took the next two frames with excellent potting to retain his title. This week’s finals: April 1 – Bill Toms final at Bugle: Matt Green v Barrie McIntosh. April 2 – Team handicap at Mevagissey: Bugle ‘B’ v St Blazey ‘A’. April 7 – Team Pairs at Mevagissey: Bethel ‘A’ v Tregonissey. April 8 – Willie Thomas at St Blazey: Ryan Tonkin v Phil Hooley. April 9 – Doubles at Lostwithiel: Gary Spencer and Andrew Best v Ryan Bilson and Barrie McIntosh.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Lostwithiel ‘A’ 0 Tregonissey ‘A’ 5 (M Knight, R Bilson (34), B McIntosh, C Stephenson, J Cocks), St Blazey ‘B’ 3 (P Baron (54), M Hancock (36), A Gooch) Bugle ‘A’ 2 (A Best, G Spencer), Bethel ‘C’ 3 (H Brett (30), P Thomas, M Powell) Bethel ‘A’ 2 (L Collard, G Hawken (36)), Mevagissey ‘B’ 1 (M Gregory) St Blazey ‘A’ 4 (L Rosevear, N Kendall, C Brown, S Dewings). Rees Memorial League: St Stephen ‘A’ v Lostwithiel ‘A’ (walkover to Lostwithiel), Bethel ‘D’ 4 (S Benallack, A Spearman, R Tonkin (31), J Pascoe), Tregonissey ‘B’ 1 (C Robins), Mevagissey ‘D’ 4 (R Blamey, S Mills, J Tomlinson, R Lakeman) Mevagissey ‘C’ 1 (M Chesterfield), Gorran Haven 3 (R Holmes, R Bunney, P Grimmett) St Stephen ‘B’ 2 (R Garside, C Nicholls). Top breaks of the week: R Bilson 34, P Baron 54, M Hancock 36, H Brett 30, G Hawken 36 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Treggy clinch the title', 'treggy-clinch-the-title', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ are champions again after beating Pelynt 4-1 to claim the title for third year running.', null, 'Mevaggisey ‘C’ went into last night’s final game against Bugle ‘C’ needing four points to take the Rees League title from Bethel ‘C’, who from their two games last week picked up seven points.', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ are champions again after beating Pelynt 4-1 to claim the title for third year running.

more Wins for Richard Pearson, Jason Simmons, Jason Cocks and Barrie McIntosh did the business with Len Flower getting the better of Martin Knight for the away point. Bugle ‘A’ claimed second place after beating St Neot 3-2 with four breaks made on the night by Gary Spencer (43), Andrew Best’s 31, Chris Perring’s 32 and Darren Lock’s 31. Bethel ‘A’ are favourites for third place after a closer than it looks 4-1 win over Mevagissey ‘B’. Mark Gregory’s final frame win leaves them three points ahead of their ‘D’ team and now sit back and wait to see how their club mates got on in their final match at Bugle ‘B’ last night to decide the relegation issue. Mevagissey ‘D’ slipped up at home losing 3-2 to basement boys Tregonissey ‘B’ for whom Simon Garrington, Phil Hooley and Andrew Gooch with a 34 got the win with Rich Lakeman and Mark Ashton replying. Chris Morford’s win over Matt Mills saw St Blazey claim the Runabout Shield at Mevagissey ‘A’ which they have to defend against the champions to keep hold of it. James Popplewell and Paul Williams took Meva’s points with Mickey Meares, Liam Reynold and Morford sealing the win. Mevaggisey ‘C’ went into last night’s final game against Bugle ‘C’ needing four points to take the Rees League title from Bethel ‘C’, who from their two games last week picked up seven points. They beat Gorran 3-2 on Tuesday with wins for Howard Brett, Leigh Rosevear an Roger Smithson with Lee McKinstry and Rob Bunney’s respot black win over Ben Stunnell replying for the home side. Also on Wednesday in a re-arranged game beat Tregonissey ‘C’ 4-1 to finish their season unless a play-off is needed. Alan Meades beat Howard Brett for Treggy’s point with wins for Stunnell, Smithson, Crockford and Rosevear, who knocked in a 33 break, took the away points. Kevin Spencer and Mo Roscorla put Bugle ‘C’ 2-0 up at home to Bethel ‘D’ who responded with victories for Jason Pascoe and Gavin Dyche to level things up leaving Roy Bayliss’s respot black win over Ross McMenemy to decide the result. Lerryn took the Runabout Shield home after beating Lostwithiel. Darren Biggen and Terry Nicholls took the home victories with John Hancock, Matt Woodward and Graham Hoskin securing the away win. Stacey Hancock got Treggy ‘C’ off to a great start at St Stephen beating Rob Garside but two blackball wins for Dan Parmenter and Anthony Harrison, along with wins for Anthony Garside and Adrian Bowyer got St Stephen a 4-1 win.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Mevagissey ‘A’ 2 (J Popplewell, P Williams) St Blazey 3 (M Meares, L Reynolds, C Morford), Bugle ‘A’ 3 (P Sturtridge, G Spencer (43), A Best (31)) St Neot 2 (C Perring (32), D Lock (31)), Tregonissey ‘A’ 4 (R Pearson, J Simmons, J Cocks, B McIntosh) Pelynt 1 (L Flower), Bethel ‘A’ 4 (M Green, A Peers, L Collard, G Stephens) Mevagissey ‘B’ 1 (M Gregory), Mevagissey ‘D’ 2 (R Lakeman, M Ashton) Tregonissey ‘B’ 3 (S Garrington, P Hooley, A Gooch (34)). Rees Memorial League: Gorran 2 (L McKinstry, R Bunney) Bethel ‘C’ 3 (H Brett, L Rosevear, R Smithon), Bugle ‘C’ 3 (K Spencer, M Roscorla, R Bayliss) Bethel ‘D’ 2 (J Pearce, G Dyche), Lostwithiel 2 (D Biggen, T Nicholls) Lerryn 3 (J Hancock, M Woodward, G Hoskin), St Stephen 4 (D Parmenter, A Harrison, A Garside, A Bowyer) Tregonissey ‘C’ 1 (S Hancock), Tregonissey ‘C’ 1 (A Meades) Bethel ‘C’ 4 (B Stunnell, R Smithson, C Crockford, L Rosevear (33)). Top breaks of the week: G Spencer 43, A Best 31, C Perring 32, D Lock 31, A Gooch 34, L Rosevear 33 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Treggy closing in on the title', 'treggy-closing-in-on-the-title', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ only needed one point at Bethel ‘A’ last night to clinch the league title after trouncing nearest rivals Mevagissey ‘A’ 5-0.', null, 'St Neot returned to winning ways with a close 3-2 win at St Blazey ‘A’. Nick Kendall and Mickey Meares who beat Darren Lock were the home winners with Steve Courts, Anthony Maddams and Cyril Dickenson on the ball for the away side.', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ only needed one point at Bethel ‘A’ last night to clinch the league title after trouncing nearest rivals Mevagissey ‘A’ 5-0.

more Martin Knight pinched the first frame off Chris Kendall to give Treggy a good start despite Chris having a 31 break. Further wins for Chris Stephenson, Ryan Bilson, Jason Cocks, who knocked in a 56 whilst defeating Matt Mills and Jason Simmons completed the rout by seeing off Phil Staley. Bugle ‘A’ have jumped into second place with a 3-2 home win against Bethel ‘A’. Bob Stephens played well to beat Mark Stuthridge but Gary Spencer, Andrew Best, who knocked in a 35, and Ian Powell put them 3-1 up only for Geoff Hawken to pip Ian Ball for Bethel’s second point. St Blazey ‘B’ finished on a high with a 5-0 victory at Pelynt with Mike Hancock knocking in a 32 break along with victories for Dave Grose, Andrew Gooch, Graham Taylor and Paul Baron. St Neot returned to winning ways with a close 3-2 win at St Blazey ‘A’. Nick Kendall and Mickey Meares who beat Darren Lock were the home winners with Steve Courts, Anthony Maddams and Cyril Dickenson on the ball for the away side. Bethel ‘C’ pushed Bugle ‘B’ all the way only to lose out by the odd frame. Ben Stunnell and Howard Brett secured the home points with Mark Sturtridge, Dave Rundle and Jake Moore successful for the Bugle boys. Champions Mevagissey ‘B’ were pushed all the way by Tregonissey ‘B’ but got home 4-1. Thanks to Ben Rothwell, Dave Kendall, Matty Rowe and Nathan Shaw with Phil Hooley beating Mark Gregory for the away side. Its so close for the runners-up spot with four teams still in the mix. Mevagissey ‘D’ got a good 3-2 win at St Stepen ‘A’ to keep alive their hopes. George Lory, Rob Blamey and Rich Lakeman got their frames with Anthony Harrison and Cary Clarke who equalled the highest break in the Rees League with a fine 38. Bethel ‘B’ sneaked past an unlucky Lerryn 3-2. James Stephens and Charles McMiram got the away points with Dave Morshead, Guy Buckle and Jim Tamblyn replying. Mevagissey ‘C’ found some late form beating St Stephen ‘B’ 5-0. Matt and Joe Chesterfield, Ben and James Prynn and predator Tregale were the marksmen.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bugle ‘A’ 3 (Ian Powell, Andrew Best (35), Gary Spencer) Bethel ‘A’ 2 (Bob Stephens, Geoff Hawken), St Blazey ‘A’ 2 (Nick Kendall, Mickey Meares) St Neot 3 (Steve Courts, Anthony Maddams, Cyril Dickenson), Tregonissey ‘A’ 5 (Martin Knight, Chris Stephenson, Jason Cocks, Jason Simmons, Ryan Bilson) Mevagissey ‘A’ 0, Bethel ‘C’ 2 (Ben Stunnell, Howard Brett) Bugle ‘B’ 3 (Mark Sturtridge, Dave Rundle, Jake Moore), Pelynt 0 St Blazey ‘B’ 5 (Dave Grose, Mike Hancock (32), Andrew Gooch, Graham Taylor, Paul Baron). Rees Memorial League: St Stephen ‘A’ 2 (Anthony Harrison, Cary Clarke (38)) Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (George Lory, Rob Blamey, Rich Lakeman), Bethel ‘B’ 3 (Dave Morshead, Guy Buckle, Jim Tamblyn) Lerryn 2 (James Stephens, Charles McMiram), Mevagissey ‘C’ 5 (Matt Chesterfield, Ben Prynn, Dave Tregale, Joe Chesterfield, James Prynn) St Stephen ‘B’ 0, Mevagissey ‘B’ 4 (Ben Rothwell, Dave Kendall, Matt Rowe, Nathan Shaw) Tregonissey ‘B’ 1 (Phil Hooley). The competition finals have also been drawn. The team pairs final between Tregonissey ‘A’ and St Blazey ‘B’ will be played at Bugle Working Men’s Club next Tuesday. Next Thursday Paul Baron will face Darren Lock in the single final at Lerryn, with Matt Mills and Paul Williams taking on Darren Lock and Steve Courts in the doubles final on April 1. The Boynton trophy will be played for a day layer at Pelynt Social Club, with Matt Green taking on Paul Baron at Mevagissey Social Club on April 4 in the Bill Tom final. Tregonissey ‘A’ will face Bugle ‘A’ in the team handicap final also at Meva a day later, with Graham Hoskin taking on Jim Tamblyn on the same night at St Blazey AFC. Leon Collard also won the Seniors final, beating Geoff Hawken 3-1. Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: A Best 35, M Hancock 32, C Clarke 38 Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Treggy closing in on title after whitewash', 'treggy-closing-in-on-title-after-whitewash', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ march on at the top of the Victory League after thumping St Neot 5-0.', null, 'Len Flowers knocked in a 33 break and Phil Ormand a 39 as Pelynt returned from St Stephen ‘A’ with a fine 4-1 win. Their other winners were Andy Parnell and Dave Chivers with Anthony Harrison beating Richard Broad to capture the home point.', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ march on at the top of the Victory League after thumping St Neot 5-0.

more Ryan Bilson beat Chris Perring in the first with Barrie McIntosh hammering Darren Lock next Martin Knight and Jason Simmons both had black ball wins over Anthony Maddams and Steve Courts respectively with Jason Cocks seeing off Cyril Dickenson in the last. Bethel ‘A’ recorded a great 4-1 win at Mevagissey ‘A’ to move into second place, Paul Baron (James Popplewell) and Garry Stephens (Leighton Varcoe) got them off to a good start but Mills beat Green in the battle of the Matts to pull one back for the home side. Andy Peers hammered Phil Staley next with Leon Collard completing the victory getting past Mark Ashton. St Blazey ‘A’ had an excellent 4-1 home win over Mevagissey ‘B’ with Micket Meares notching a 34 clearance when beating Matt Rowe and Aiden Maxwell-Craddock knocking in a fine 38 against Nathan Shaw and their were further wins for Stewart Dewings and Nick Kendall who beat Dave Kendall. Mark Gregory’s good form continued as he saw off Mark Craddock for the away points. Len Flowers knocked in a 33 break and Phil Ormand a 39 as Pelynt returned from St Stephen ‘A’ with a fine 4-1 win. Their other winners were Andy Parnell and Dave Chivers with Anthony Harrison beating Richard Broad to capture the home point. Mevagissey ‘D’ have really opened up the Rees League title race after hammering leaders Tregonissey ‘B’ 4-1. Wins for George Lory, Mark Ashton (35 break), Rich Lakeman and Scott Mills saw them move into second place in the league with Mike Hooley replying for the leaders. Bethel ‘B’ had to hand over the Runabout Shield to their ‘D’ team after a 3-2 defeat. Lee Searle (Rich Bolt), Jason Pascoe (Martin Isted) and Ryan Tonkin (Jim Tamblyn) put the ‘D’ side 3-0 up. Guy Buckle pulled one back getting past Ross McMenemy and Clive Stuthridge managed to get their second point defeating Dave Morshead who led right up until the black was potted. There was a long night on the other table as the ‘C’ team slipped to a suprise defeat to Lerryn. Howard Brett’s 30 break and a fine potting performance from Roger Smithson put them 2-0 up. There then followed an 85-minute frame between Leigh Rosevear and Charles McMiram which Charles nicked to start the comeback. Courtney Crockford led all the way in his frame only for Terry Middleton to pip him and Graham Hoskin defeated Ben Stunnell to complete the comeback. Gorran Haven completed a great win over Mevagissey ‘C’. Wins for Lee McKinstry, Rob Bunney and Paul Grimmett, who pipped the Predator and Andy Reddall got their four points with Adrian Evans seeing off Geoff Hayhurst for the away side. St Stephen ‘B’ are flying high after a whitewash of Tregonissey ‘C’ to reach their best position ever. Rob Garside was run close by Michaela Edwards and Darren Biggen by Jacqui Hancock with other wins for Terry and Craig Nicholls and Josh Doherty.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: St Blazey ‘A’ 4 (M Meares, S Dewings, N Kendall, A Maxwell-Craddock (38)) Mevagissey ‘B’ 1 (M Gregory), Mevagissey ‘A’ 1 (M Mills) Bethel ‘A’ 4 (P Baron, G Stephens, A Peers, L Collard), Tregonissey ‘A’ 5 (R Bilson, B McIntosh, M Knight, J Simmons, J Cocks) St Neot 0, St Stephen ‘A’ 1 (A Harrison) Pelynt 4 (A Parnell, L Flower (33), D Chivers, P Ormand (39)). Rees Memorial League: Bethel ‘C’ 2 (H Brett, R Smithson) Lerryn 3 (C McMiram, T Middleton, G Hoskin), Gorran Haven 4 (L McKinstry, R Bunney, P Grimmett, A Reddall) Mevagissey ‘C’ 1 (A Evans), Mevagissey ‘D’ 4 (G Lory, M Ashton (35), R Lakeman, S Mills) Tregonissey ‘B’ 1 (P Hooley), Tregonissey ‘C’ 0 St Stephen ‘B’ 3, Bethel ‘B’ 2 Bethel ‘D’ 3. Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: A Maxwell-Craddock 38, L Flower 33, M Meares 34, P Ormand 39 The draw for the next round of all the competitions was made by Matt Rowe and Mark Gregory at Mevagissey Social Club. Seniors: Philip Ormand v Geoff Hawken, Leon Collard v Len Flower. Singles: Barrie McIntosh v Steve Courts, Ryan Bilson v James Popplewell, Gary Stephens v Andrew Best, Darren Lock v Andy Peers. Bill Toms: Barrie McIntosh v Adrian Bowyer, Darren Lock v Ryan Bilson, Jason Simmons v Andrew Best, Jason Cocks v Philip Hooley. Willie Thomas: Philip Hooley v Jim Tamblyn, Stacey Hancock v Leigh Rosevear, Clive Stuthridge v Justin Hooley, Courtney Crockford v George Lory. Doubles: J Popplewell and P Staley v M Knight and M Woodward, M Green and C Stuthridge v S Courts and D Lock, A Best and G Spencer v G Hawken and A Peers, G Stephens and R Opie v J Cocks and J Simmons. Team Pairs: Bugle ‘B’ v Bugle ‘A’, Bethel ‘A’ v Tregonissey ‘A’. To be played on Tuesday, February 7. Team Handicap: St Blazey ‘A’ v Bethel ‘C’ to be played on Tuesday, February 7, Bugle ‘B’ v Bethel ‘A’ date TBA. Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Treggy move clear at the top of the Victory League', 'treggy-move-clear-at-the-top-of-the-victory-league', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ went four points clear at the top of the Victory League after trashing Mevagissey ‘A’ 4-1 only James Popplewell, who whitewashed Martin Knight 79-0, which involved a 47 break, could do anything as the…', null, 'Pelynt got a good 3-2 home win over Bugle ‘B’ to stay well clear of the relegation places. Andy Parnell and Mark Sturtridge traded frames before Phil Broad and John Daniell put the home side 2-0 up. Only for Tony Conroy to beat Len Flower in the last.', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ went four points clear at the top of the Victory League after trashing Mevagissey ‘A’ 4-1 only James Popplewell, who whitewashed Martin Knight 79-0, which involved a 47 break, could do anything as the defending champions set out their stall.

more Bethel ‘A’ moved into second place courtesy of a 3-2 win at Mevagissey ‘B’. Gary Stephens’ fine 38 break got them underway and Paul Baron doubled the lead sneaking past Mark Gregory. Matt Rowe’s respot win over Andy Peers and Joe Chesterfield’s thrashing of Geoff Hawken levelled things up only for Leon Collard to see off Dave Kendall for the away win. Pelynt got a good 3-2 home win over Bugle ‘B’ to stay well clear of the relegation places. Andy Parnell and Mark Sturtridge traded frames before Phil Broad and John Daniell put the home side 2-0 up. Only for Tony Conroy to beat Len Flower in the last. St Stephen ‘A’ and St Neot was postponed. In the Rees League leaders Tregonissey ‘B’ could only must four players at Mevagissey ‘C’ and suffered their second defeat of the season 3-2. Father and son Philip and Justin put them 2-0 up before Joe Chesterfield and Charlie Prynn levelled up giving Dave Kendall a walkover in the last. Bethel B’s 4-1 home win over St Stephen ‘B’ moves them second but the league is so tight with six point separating the top six. Guy Buckle and Richie Bolt put Bethel 2-0 up but Terry Nicholls beat Jim Tamblyn to pull a frame back but Clive Stuthridge and Martin Isted’s black ball win over Craig Nicholls saw them home. Mevagissey ‘D’s whitewash of Tregonissey ‘C’ sees them move up to third and looking a real threat. Jacqui Hancock pushed Rob Blamey all the way in the first but easy wins for Rich Lakeman, George, Ian Latham and Mark Ashton saw Meva pick up the nap hand. Bethel ‘D’ sit sixth after beating Lerryn 4-1. They ran up the first four games through Lee Searle, Andy Spearman, Dave Morsehead and Jason Pascoe but James Stephens stopped the rot comfortably getting the better of Ross McMenemy.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Tregonissey ‘A’ 4 (R Bilson, J Simmons, J Cocks, B McIntosh) Mevagissey ‘A’ 1 (J Popplewell (47)), Pelynt 3 (A Parnell, P Broad, J Daniell) Bugle ‘B’ 2 (M Stuthridge, T Conroy), Mevagissey ‘B’ 2 (J Chesterfield, M Rowe) Bethel ‘A’ 3 (G Stephens (38), P Baron, L Collard). St Stephen ‘A’ v St Neot – postponed. Rees Memorial League: Tregonissey ‘C’ 0 Mevagissey ‘D’ 5 (R Blamey, R Lakeman, W George, I Latham, M Ashton), Bethel ‘B’ 4 (G Buckle, R Bolt, C Stuthridge, M Isted) St Stephen ‘B’ 1 (T Nicholls), Bethel ‘D’ 4 (L Searle, A Spearman, D Morshead, J Pascoe) Lerryn 1 (J Stephens), Mevagissey ‘C’ 3 (J Chesterfield, C Prynn, D Kendall w/o) Tregonissey ‘B’ (J Hooley, P Hooley). Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: J Popplewell 47, G Stephens 38 The draw for the next round of all the competitions was made by Matt Rowe and Mark Gregory at Mevagissey Social Club. Seniors: Philip Ormand v Geoff Hawken, Leon Collard v Len Flower. Singles: Barrie McIntosh v Steve Courts, Ryan Bilson v James Popplewell, Gary Stephens v Andrew Best, Darren Lock v Andy Peers. Bill Toms: Barrie McIntosh v Adrian Bowyer, Darren Lock v Ryan Bilson, Jason Simmons v Andrew Best, Jason Cocks v Philip Hooley. Willie Thomas: Philip Hooley v Jim Tamblyn, Stacey Hancock v Leigh Rosevear, Clive Stuthridge v Justin Hooley, Courtney Crockford v George Lory. Doubles: J Popplewell and P Staley v M Knight and M Woodward, M Green and C Stuthridge v S Courts and D Lock, A Best and G Spencer v G Hawken and A Peers, G Stephens and R Opie v J Cocks and J Simmons. Team Pairs: Bugle ‘B’ v Bugle ‘A’, Bethel ‘A’ v Tregonissey ‘A’. To be played on Tuesday, February 7. Team Handicap: St Blazey ‘A’ v Bethel ‘C’ to be played on Tuesday, February 7, Bugle ‘B’ v Bethel ‘A’ date TBA. Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Treggy move to the top of the tree', 'treggy-move-to-the-top-of-the-tree', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ moved back to the top of the Victory League after blowing away Pelynt 4-1.', null, 'Mevagissey ‘D’ have jumped up to seventh after beating basement boys Treggy ‘C’ 4-1. Andrew Gooch had a 36 break beating Rich Lakeman, but good wins for James Tomlinson, Scott Mills, Mark Ashton and Ian Latham did the business for the Meva boys.', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ moved back to the top of the Victory League after blowing away Pelynt 4-1.

more Big wins for Ryan Bilson, Jason Cocks and Rich Pearson who knocked in a 35 break followed by a 61 clearance by Barrie McIntosh when trailing Phil Ormond got their points with Richard Broad hammering Martin Knight for the home side. Bugle ‘A’ got three points at St Neot as Jake Moore’s black-ball victory over Chris Perring followed by Gary Spencer who knocked in a 33 whilst defeating Anthony Maddams. Steve Courts stopped the rot knocking in a 47 to give Andrew Best his first defeat of the season. Ian Ball’s win over Matt Vessey sealed the victory with Darren Lock’s win over Paul Sturtridge concluding the evening. Bethel ‘A’ got all five points at Mevagissey ‘B’ all frames were tight especially Matt Green’s respot black win over Ben Rothwell. The visitors just had enough on the night to win. Great win for St Blazey at home to Mevagissey ‘A’ 3-2. James Popplewell started with a 39 break for the visitors with Mickey Meares replying with a 30 to level things. Steward Dewings beat Matt Mills for the home side with Phil Staley replying, beating Liam Reynolds which left Nick Kendall’s 86-9 victory over Leighton Varcoe to finish the evening. Mevagissey ‘D’ have jumped up to seventh after beating basement boys Treggy ‘C’ 4-1. Andrew Gooch had a 36 break beating Rich Lakeman, but good wins for James Tomlinson, Scott Mills, Mark Ashton and Ian Latham did the business for the Meva boys. Andy Reddall of Gorran was so confident of beating Ben Stunnell he promised to buy his teammates a pint if he lost. A 44-2 defeat meant that his wallet had to come out. His teammates did no better with Bethel ‘C’ moved to the top of the table with five points at Mevagissey ‘C’ had the night off. Natalie Rosevear defeated Rob Garside to get Tregonissey ‘C’s point at home to St Stephen, who remain third wins for Dan Parminter, Chris Brown, Antony Garside and Ady Bowyer sees them handily placed for a title challenge after Christmas. Lerryn and Lostwithiel had a very tight encounter with the home side edging it 3-2. Matt Woodward, John Hancock and Terry Middleton were their winners with Craig Nicholls and Darren Biggens replying. Bethel ‘D’ and Bugle ‘C’ fought out another close one with two single point wins. Dayle Dyche beating Nigel Couchman with Mo Roscorla pipping Jason Pascoe. Dave Morshed and Gavin Dyche got two more points for the home side with Kevin Spencer getting Bugle’s other point.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: St Blazey 3 (M Meares, S Dewings, N Kendall) Mevagissey ‘A’ 2 (J Popplewell, P Staley), St Neot 2 (S Courts (47), D Lock) Bugle ‘A’ 3 (J Moore, G Spencer (33), I Ball), Pelynt 1 Tregonissey ‘A’ 4, Mevagissey ‘B’ 0 Bethel ‘A’ 5 (A Peers, G Hawken, M Green, L Collard, G Stephens), Tregonissey ‘B’ 1 (A Gooch (36)) Mevagissey ‘D’ 4 (J Tomlinson, S Mills, M Ashton, I Latham). Rees Memorial League: Bethel ‘C’ 5 (H Brett, P Thomas, B Stunnell, C Crockford, L Rosevear) Gorran 0, Bethel ‘D’ 3 (D Dyche, D Morshead, G Dyche) Bugle ‘C’ 2 (K Spencer, M Roscorla), Lerryn 3 (M Woodward, J Hancock, T Middleton) Lostwithiel 2 (C Nicholls, D Biggen), Tregonissey ‘C’ 1 (N Rosevear) St Stephen 4 (D Parminter, C Brown, A Garside, A Bowyer). Top breaks of the week: G Spencer 33, S Courts 47, A Gooch 36, R Pearson 35, B McIntosh 61 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Treggy open up six-point gap at the top of the table', 'treggy-open-up-six-point-gap-at-the-top-of-the-table', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'It’s been a great week for Tregonissey ‘A’ as they opened up a six-point lead at the top of the Victory League as a result of their 5-0 win at Mevagissey ‘B’ helped by Barry Mcintosh''s 30 and Martin Knight''s 37 breaks.', null, '"Bethel ‘C’ moved off the bottom of the league by potting two points at home to St Blazey ‘A’. Paul Thomas and Martin Powell on target for the home side and Chris Brown, Mickey Meares and Nick Kendall replying."', 'It’s been a great week for Tregonissey ‘A’ as they opened up a six-point lead at the top of the Victory League as a result of their 5-0 win at Mevagissey ‘B’ helped by Barry Mcintosh''s 30 and Martin Knight''s 37 breaks.

more They now look clear favourites at this early stage. They were certainly helped by second place Mevagissey ‘A’ crashing away to to a 4-1 defeat at Lostwithiel ‘A’. Cyril Dickinson''s colour clear-up against Pete Chambers, followed by wins for Darren Lock, Steve Courts and David Grose who narrowly defeated Phil Stacey helped inflict Meva''s first defeat of the season. Mike Hancock''s superb 72 break and Graham Taylor''s 31 helped St Blazey ‘B’ to defeat Bugle ‘B’ 4-1 with Ian Ball stopping the rot by beating Andrew Geach. Bethel ‘C’ moved off the bottom of the league by potting two points at home to St Blazey ‘A’. Paul Thomas and Martin Powell on target for the home side and Chris Brown, Mickey Meares and Nick Kendall replying. Three great wins for Lostwithiel ‘B’, Bethel ‘D’ and Mevagissey ‘C’ has set them aside at the top of the Rees league, Paul Grimmett played well to see off Andy Parnell but John Daniel, George Lory, Richard Broad and Lenny Flower won to keep Lostwithiel ‘B’ one point clear of Bethel ‘D’. They had an excellent 4-1 victory at Tregonissey ‘B’. Highlights included Jason Pascoe''s excellent victory over Richard Opie, with Andy Spearman, Shaun Hicks and Ryan Tonkin also winning. Justin Hooley continued his excellent season so far getting Treggy''s point. Now the focus is on the top two when they play next week. Mevagissey''s ‘C’ 5-0 whitewash of Lerryn sees them jumping right up to third place although they have played a game more than the top two. Predator Dave Tregales ‘ mid-frame 22 break made all the difference in defeating John Hancock, while Ben Prynn''s pot of the final black was described by the watching crowd as ''the shot of the night’. There were four very tight frames as Bethel ‘B’ beat St Stephen ‘A’ with Guy Buckles’ excellent victory over Cary Clark coupled with wins for Rich Allen and Clive Stuthridge seeing them home with Anthony Harrison and Anthony Garside replying. Graham Rosevear''s victory over M Hodge was St Stephen ‘B’s only bright spot as they went down 1-4 at home to Bugle ‘C’ with Wingrave, Couchman, Jenkin and Spencer taking the points back to Bugle. This week saw the start of the preliminary round of the team pairs played with league games returning next week.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bethel ‘C’ 2 (P Thomas, M Powell) St Blazey ‘A’ 3 (C Brown, M Meares, N Kendall), St Blazey ‘B’ 4 (D Kirwan, M Hancock (72), G Taylor (31), P Baron) Bugle ‘B’ 1 (I Ball), Lostwithiel ‘A’ 4 (C Dickinson, D Lock, S Courts, D Grose) Mevagissey ‘A’ 1 (P Williams), Mevagissey ‘A’ 0 Tregonissey ‘A’ 5 (R Bilson, C Stephenson, M Knight (37), B McIntosh (30), J Cocks). Rees Memorial League: Bethel ‘B’ 3 (G Buckle, R Allen, C Stuthridge) St Stephen ‘A’ 2 (A Harrison, A Garside), St Stephen ‘B’ 1 (G Rosevear) Bugle ‘C’ 4 (S Wingrave, N Couchman, P Jenkin, K Spencer), Gorran Haven 1 (P Grimmett) Lostwithiel 4 (J Daniell, G Lory, R Broad, L Flower), Mevagissey ‘C’ 5 (J Prynn, D Tregale, M Chesterfield, J Chesterfield, B Prynn) Lerryn 0, Tregonissey ‘B’ 1 (J Hooley) Bethel ‘D’ 4 (J Pascoe, R Tonkin, S Hicks, A Spearman). Top breaks of the week: M Hancock 72, M Knight 37, G Taylor 31, B McIntosh 30 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Treggy pip Legion to second', 'treggy-pip-legion-to-second', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'It was nip and tuck all night in the race to be league runners-up which saw Tregonissey pinch it after they and Tywardreath ‘A’ both triumph 4-1, leaving Treggy one point ahead.', null, '"Mevagissey ‘C’ took the notable scalp of champions St Blazey 3-2 in a night of high quality snooker with David Tregales. Pink and black finish talked about long into the nighty, the result clinched third spot for the Meva boys."', 'It was nip and tuck all night in the race to be league runners-up which saw Tregonissey pinch it after they and Tywardreath ‘A’ both triumph 4-1, leaving Treggy one point ahead.

more Mike Hancock’s fine 62 and Barry McIntosh’s 30 clearances to the fore on the evening for Treggy. Lostwithiel ‘B’ condemned Sparks to the Rees League after a convincing 4-1 victory with Bryan Warne and George Lory getting the better of Richie Bolt and Steve Bampton respectively in the evening’s crucial frame. St Neot finished a good season convincingly despatching Mevagissey ‘A’ 4-1 whilst the popular Mevagissey ‘B’ went out of the league with a bang a they saw off Bethel ‘A’ 3-2 and hopefully a quick return to the top division won’t be long. Mevagissey ‘C’ took the notable scalp of champions St Blazey 3-2 in a night of high quality snooker with David Tregales. Pink and black finish talked about long into the nighty, the result clinched third spot for the Meva boys. Lerryn produced the result of the night as Graham Hoskin, Terry Middleton and Dave Phipps clinched a notable victory over Bugle ‘C’ and Tywardreath ‘B’ finished the season strongly beating Gorran Haven 4-1 with Lee McKinstry on target as usual for the Haven. While Scott Mills played very well to record Mevagissey ‘D’s only point at runners-up Bethel ‘D’.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: St Neot 4 (M Courts, N Collins, M Rowe, S Courts) Mevagissey ‘A’ 1 (R Lakeman), Sparks 1 (M Williams) Lostwithiel ‘B’ 4 (B Warne, G Lory, J Hill, L Flower), Mevagissey ‘B’ 3 (M Mills, D Kendall, B Rothwell) Bethel ‘A’ 2 (G Stephens, G Hawken), Bugle ‘B’ 1 (G Taylor) Tywardreath ‘A’ 4 (A Gooch, M Hancock (62), P Baron, L Sturtridge), Lostwithiel ‘A’ 1 (D Lock) Tregonissey 4 (C Kendall, C Stephenson, B McIntosh (30), R Pearson). Rees Memorial League: Bethel ‘D’ 4 (V Hathway, T Stephen, R Tonkin, J Pascoe) Mevagissey ‘D’ 1 (S Mills), Tywardreath ‘B’ 4 (G Kent, K Wilks, A Dennis, N Rowe) Gorran Haven 1 (L McKinstry), Mevagissey ‘C’ 3 (M Chesterfield, D Tregale, J Chesterfield) St Blazey 2 (M Woodward, M Meares), Bugle ‘C’ 2 (N Couchman, K Moore) Lerryn 3 (G Hoskin, J Middleton, D Phipps). Top breaks of the week: M Hancock 62, B McIntosh 30 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Treggy pull clear at the top', 'treggy-pull-clear-at-the-top', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ put on an impressive display taking four points off Bugle ‘A’ to move five points clear at the top of the St Blazey and District Snooker League’s Victory League.', null, 'Bugle ‘B’ moved up a place after beating Tregonissey ‘B’, Wins for Colin and Dave Rundle along with Keith Moore saw them through with Justin Hooley and Andrew Gooch replying.', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ put on an impressive display taking four points off Bugle ‘A’ to move five points clear at the top of the St Blazey and District Snooker League’s Victory League.

more Wins for Richard Pearson, Barrie McIntosh, who knocked in breaks of 53 and 30, Jason Simmons and Ryan Bilson saw them home, with Andrew Best beating Jason Cocks for Bugle’s point. Mevagissey ‘B’ have dropped into the bottom two after losing out to their ‘A’ team by four games to one. Dave Kendall took out Leighton Varcoe in the opener but that was as good as it got for them as Matt Mills, Phil Staley, Paul Williams and James Popplewell took the ‘A’ team points. Williams and Popplewell both knocking in breaks of 37. In the next room at Meva, the ‘D’ team are thriving and took an excellent four points against Pelynt. Mark Ashton, Rich Lakeman, James Tomlinson and Rob Blamey were their marksmen with Scott Mills getting the bad draw and lost out to the classy Phil Ormond. St Blazey picked up a great 3-2 win at St Neot, with wins for Liam Reynolds, Stewart Dewings and Chris Morford. The home side replied through Steve Courts and Darren Lock, who had a 37 break. Bugle ‘B’ moved up a place after beating Tregonissey ‘B’, Wins for Colin and Dave Rundle along with Keith Moore saw them through with Justin Hooley and Andrew Gooch replying. Mevagissey ‘C’ helped themselves to four points at St Stephen and now lead the Rees League by two points. A great win by Adrian Evans over Chris Brown got them going with Ben Prynn, Dave Tregale and Matt Chesterfield also recording wins. Anthony Garside defeated Joe Chesterfield for the home point. Gavin Dyche’s black ball win over Roger Smithson got Bethel ‘D’ off to a good start against their ‘C’ team. Howard Brett levelled things, helped a lot by four points by Dave Morshead. Dayle Dyche and Jason Pascoe then put the ‘D’ team 3-1 up with Ben Stunnell seeing off Ross McMenemy for their second point. Bethel ‘B’ moved up to third after taking four points at Lostwithiel through Richie Bolt, Clive Stuthridge, Guy Buckle and Martin Isted. John Wilson took the home point through a walkover. John Hancock meanwhile beat debutant Mark Wherry to put Lerryn ahead at home to Gorran. Rob Bunney and Lee McKinstry put the away side ahead with Terry Middleton squaring things up leaving Steve Williams to beat Charles McMiram for the away side.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Mevagissey ‘B’ 1 (D Kendall) Mevagissey ‘A’ 4 (M Mills, P Staley, P Williams (37), J Popplewell (37)), Tregonissey ‘A’ 4 (R Pearson, B McIntosh (53&30), J Simmons, R Bilson) Bugle ‘A’ 1 (A Best), Mevagissey ‘D’ 4 (M Ashton, R Lakeman, J Tomlinson, R Blamey) Pelynt 1 (P Ormond), St Neot 2 (S Courts, D Lock (37)) St Blazey 3 (L Reynolds, S Dewings, C Morford), Bugle ‘B’ 3 (C Rundle, K Moore, D Rundle) Tregonissey ‘B’ 2 (J Hooley, A Gooch). Rees Memorial League: Lerryn 2 (J Hancock, T Middleton) Gorran 3 (R Bunney, L McKinstry, S Williams), St Stephen 1 (A Garside) Mevagissey ‘C’ 4 (A Evans, B Prynn, D Tregale, M Chesterield), Bethel ‘D’ 3 (G Dyche, D Dyche, J Pascoe) Bethel ‘C’ 2 (H Brett, B Stunnell), Lostwithiel 1 (J Wilton) Bethel ‘B’ 4 (R Bolt, C Stuthridge, G Buckle, M Isted). Top breaks of the week: P Williams 37, J Popplewell 37, B McIntosh 53,30, D Lock 37 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Treggy take full advantage of Meva''s week off', 'treggy-take-full-advantage-of-mevas-week-off', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ took full advantage of Mevagissey ‘A’s free week by drawing level with them at the top of the Victory League with a 5-0 win over St Blazey ‘A’.', null, 'In the Rees League nothing is going to stop Mevagissey ‘B’ winning it as they beat Lerryn 5-0 and now stand 13 points clear.', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ took full advantage of Mevagissey ‘A’s free week by drawing level with them at the top of the Victory League with a 5-0 win over St Blazey ‘A’.

more So the Meva boys will be hoping to take maximum points against Bethel ‘C’ last night on Treggy’s night off. There was a real shock at Bugle as the ‘B’ team took three frames on the black to really knock their ‘A’ team’s chances of finishing top. Mark Sturtridge, Dave Rundle and Colin Rundle were their heroes with Ian Powell and Andrew Best replying. Dave Grose had a dizzy moment in his frame against Andy Spearman and took a break with a clearance of 35 when he was 40 points behind – only he knows why but it was Bethel ‘D’s only point as St Blazey ‘B’ completed a 4-1 win through Baron, Taylor, Hancock and Gooch. The only other break was a noteable one as Bethel ‘A’ returned from St Neot with a 4-1 victory. Steve Courts gave the home side a good start beating Leon Collard but wins for Gary Stephens (Cyril Dickinson), Geoff Hawken (Anthony Maddams), Matt Green, with a 44 when beating Darren Lock, and Andy Peers (Chris Perring) gave the Bethel boys a very enjoyable drive home. Martin Powell and captain Roger Smithson got basement boys Bethel ‘C’ two points against visiting Pelynt who replied through Paul Ormond, Len Flower and Richard Broad. In the Rees League nothing is going to stop Mevagissey ‘B’ winning it as they beat Lerryn 5-0 and now stand 13 points clear. St Stephen ‘B’ took two frames off their ‘A’ side with Craig Nicholls beating John Bennett on the black in the final frame to get their second point. The match of the evening was at Mevagissey ‘C’ as they beat Bodmin Q-Bar. Matt Chesterfield (Marcus Meneer) and Steve Bampton (Charlie Prynn) traded frames with Charlie potting the black but going in off and the predator Tregale lost on a Richard Bolt special, black five times around the table and fluked it in the middle bag. Ben Prynn and Joe Chesterfield took the last two frames to get the home side home. Bethel ‘B’ completed a 4-1 away win over Lerryn with a re-arranged game on Monday night.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bugle ‘B’ 3 (Mark Sturtridge, Dave Rundle, Colin Rundle) Bugle ‘A’ 2 (Ian Powell, Andrew Best), Tregonissey ‘A’ 5 (Ryan Bilson, Jason Simmons, Jason Cocks, Martin Knight, Chris Stephenson) St Blazey ‘A’ 0, St Neot 1 (Steve Courts) Bethel ‘A’ 4 (Gary Stephens, Geoff Hawken, Matt Green (44), Andy Peers), St Blazey ‘A’ 4 (Paul Baron, Graham Taylor, Mike Hanock, Andrew Gooch) Bethel ‘D’ 1 (Andy Spearman), Bethel ‘C’ 2 (Martin Powell, Roger Smithson) Pelynt 3 (Phil Ormond, Len Flower, Richard Broad). Rees Memorial League: St Stephen ‘B’ 2 (Terry Nicholls, Craig Nicholls) St Stephen ‘A’ 3 (Cary Clarke, Anthony Garside, Dan Allen), Mevagissey ‘B’ 5 (Nathan Shaw, Mark Gregory, Ben Rothwell, Dave Kendall, Matt Rowe), Mevagissey ‘C’ 3 (Matt Chesterfield, Ben Prynn, Joe Chesterfield) Bodmin Q-Bar 2 (Steve Bampton, Richie Bolt), Lerryn 1 (Trevor Pearce) Bethel ‘B’ 4 (Mike Williams, Clive Stuthridge, Rich Allen, Dave Morshead). Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: M Green 44, D Grose 35 Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Treggy two points away from the title', 'treggy-two-points-away-from-the-title', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ and Mevagissey ‘B’ both need only two points each to claim the winners and runners-up trophies after the latest round of play-off matches this week.', null, '"Bethel ‘A’ were surprise 5-0 winners at St Blazey ‘A’. Bob Stephens, who was making his first appearance of the season, Geoff Hawken, Matt Green, Gary Stephens and Leon ‘Walking Stick’ Collard all winning."', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ and Mevagissey ‘B’ both need only two points each to claim the winners and runners-up trophies after the latest round of play-off matches this week.

more Treggy claimed a 3-2 home win over Bugle ‘A’. Andrew Best and Gary Spencer did their bit to stop the champions elect but wins for Martin Knight, Jason Cocks and Barrie McIntosh (49) saw them home. Mevagissey ‘A’ came away from Lostwithiel ‘A’ with a 3-2 victory. Pete Chambers’ first frame win over Chris Perring was followed by two wins for the hosts for Darren Lock, with a 35, and Steve Courts but Paul Williams’ 45 and Phil Staley saw Meva home. Mevagissey ‘B’ upset the form book when beating Bugle ‘B’ 3-2. Leighton Varcoe’s 35, Ben Rothwell and Nathan Shaw took their frames as they moved level with Bethel ‘C’ at the bottom of the table – Ian Ball and Jake Moore replied for Bugle. Bethel ‘A’ were surprise 5-0 winners at St Blazey ‘A’. Bob Stephens, who was making his first appearance of the season, Geoff Hawken, Matt Green, Gary Stephens and Leon ‘Walking Stick’ Collard all winning. St Blazey bounced back the following night by beating Tregonissey ‘A’ 3-2 in the team handicap semi-final. In the Rees League champions Lostwithiel ‘A’ triumphed 4-1 at Tregonissey ‘B’. Phil Hooley took Treggy’s point beating John Daniell but George Lory, Richard Broad, Len Flower (32) and Andy Parnell saw them home. Bethel ‘D’ are clear favourites for the runners-up spot after beating their ‘B’ team 3-2. Steve Benallack, Jason Pascoe and Ryan Tonkin (32) saw them home with Jim Tamblyn and Guy Buckle replying. Mevagissey ‘C’s recent good form continued as they brushed aside St Stephen ‘B’ 4-1. Rob Garside won for the home beating Matt Chesterfield but Ben Prynn, John Prynn, Joe Chesterfield and the Predator took the points back to Meva. Gorran Haven moved to within a point of Lerryn and Bugle ‘C’ after beating Lerryn 3-2. Courtesy of wins for Rob Bunney, Richard Holmes and Lee McKinstry with Terry Middleton and Graham Hoskin replying.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Lostwithiel ‘A’ 2 (D Locks (35), S Courts) Mevagissey ‘A’ 3 (P Chambers, P Williams (45), P Staley), Tregonissey ‘A’ 3 (M Knight, B McIntosh, J Cocks) Bugle ‘A’ 2 (G Spencer, A Best), Mevagissey ‘B’ 3 (L Varcoe (34), B Rothwell, N Shaw) Bugle ‘B’ 2 (I Ball, J Moore), St Blazey ‘A’ 0 Bethel ‘A’ 5 (B Stephens, G Hawken, M Green, G Stephens, L Collard). Rees Memorial League: Bethel ‘D’ 3 (S Benallack, J Pascoe, R Tonkin (32) Bethel ‘B’ 2 (J Tamblyn, G Buckle), Tregonissey ‘B’ 1 (P Hooley) Lostwithiel ‘B’ 4 (G Lory, R Broad, L Flower (32), A Parnell), Gorran Haven 2 (R Bunney, R Holmes, L McKinstry) Lerryn 3 (T Middleton, G Hoskin), St Stephen ‘B’ 1 (R Garside) Mevagissey ‘C’ 4 (J Prynn, D Tregale, J Chesterfield, B Prynn). Top breaks of the week: B McIntosh 49, D Lock 35, P Williams 45, L Flower 32, R Tonkin 32 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Tregonissey clinch the Victory League', 'tregonissey-clinch-the-victory-league', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ clinched the Victory League title with a game to spare after beating close rivals Bugle ‘A’ 3-2.', null, 'Mevagissey ‘B’ are deep in trouble after another disappointing display at Pelynt going down 4-1. Only Ben Rothwell’s victory over Will Courts gave them any rewards as the home side romped home through Jan Daniell, with a 27 clearance, Richard Broad, Phil Ormand and Andy Parnell.', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ clinched the Victory League title with a game to spare after beating close rivals Bugle ‘A’ 3-2.

more Martin Knight’s respot win over Andrew Best was followed by a 56 clearance from Barrie McIntosh to see off Gary Spencer. Ryan Bilson put them three up beating Ian Ball but Bugle pulled the last two frames back through Jake Moore and Paul Sturtridge. Second-placed Bethel ‘A’ picked up a 3-2 win at Bugle ‘B’ to sit four points above Bugle ‘A’ but having played a game more. Gary Stephens took out Mark Sturtridge in the opener which was followed with Matt Green’s win over Tony Conroy which included a 31 break. Dave and Colin Rundle levelled things up beating Geoff Hawken and Andy Peers respectively, but Leon Collard’s win over Keith Moore in the last gave Bethel the win. Relegated St Stephen ‘A’ were 2-0 down at home to St Blazey ‘A’ after wins for Stewart Dewings and Mickey Meares but convincing wins for Anthony Garside and Dan Allen left Anthony Harrison’s black-ball win over Aiden Maxwell Craddock secured the win. Mevagissey ‘B’ are deep in trouble after another disappointing display at Pelynt going down 4-1. Only Ben Rothwell’s victory over Will Courts gave them any rewards as the home side romped home through Jan Daniell, with a 27 clearance, Richard Broad, Phil Ormand and Andy Parnell. The Rees League title would have been settled last night as Mevagissey ‘D’ and Tregonissey ‘B’ played their last games. The Meva boys slipped to a 3-2 home defeat to Bethel ‘C’. Howard Brett saw off Rich Lakeman in the first win Roger Smithson winning the second frame despite George Lory’s 34 break. Mark Ashton pulled one back by beating Courtney Crockford only for Leigh Rosevear’s win over Ian Latham securing the away win leaving Rob Blamey’s victory over Ben Stunnell to give the Meva boys a one-point lead at the top. Graham Hoskin’s win over Simon Garrington was Lerryn’s only success as Richard Opie, Phil, Mike and Justin Hooley were all winners as Treggy ‘B’ closed in on Meva waiting for any slip up from them. Bethel ‘B’ remain third after a 4-1 home win over Gorran. Clive Stuthridge, Richard Bolt, Guy Buckle and Jim Tamblyn’s black ball win over Lee McKinstry got their points with Rob Bunney’s win over Martin Isted Gorran’s only success. Dave Morshead and Andy Spearman put Bethel ‘D’ 2-0 up at home to Mevagissey ‘C’ who replied when Predator Tregale beat Lee Searle, Ross McMenemy restored Bethel’s two frame lead beating Joe Chesterfield leaving Matt Chesterfield victory over Jason Pascoe to get Meva’s other point.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Tregonissey ‘A’ 3 (M Knight, B McIntosh (56 clearance), R Bilson) Bugle ‘A’ 2 (J Moore, P Sturtridge), St Stephen ‘A’ 3 (A Garside, D Allen, A Harrison) St Blazey ‘A’ 2 (S Dewings, M Meares), Pelynt 4 (J Daniell, R Broad, P Ormand, A Parnell) Mevagissey ‘B’ 1 (B Rothwell), Bugle ‘B’ 2 (D Rundle, C Rundle) Bethel ‘A’ 3 (G Stephens, M Green (31), L Collard). Rees Memorial League: Mevagissey ‘D’ 2 (M Ashton, R Blamey) Bethel ‘C’ 3 (H Brett, R Smithson, L Rosevear), Bethel ‘B’ 4 (C Stuthridge, R Bolt, J Tamblyn, G Buckle) Gorran 1 (R Bunney), Bethel ‘D’ 3 (D Morshead, A Speaman, R McMenemy) Mevagissey ‘C’ 2 (D Tregale, M Chesterfield), Lerryn 1 (G Hoskin) Tregonissey ‘B’ 4 (R Opie, P Hooley, M Hooley, J Hooley). Top breaks of the week: B McIntosh 56,M Green 31 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Tregonissey close in on the Victory League title', 'tregonissey-close-in-on-the-victory-league-title', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ need a maximum of 13 points to regain Victory League title after getting all five points at Mevagissey ‘B’.', null, 'Chris Morford and Nick Kendall, who knocked in a 31 break, put St Blazey 2-0 up at Tregonissey ‘B’ who fought back with wins for Mike Hooley and Andrew Gooch with Dean Willis beating Justin Hooley in the last to clinch victory for the Blazey boys.', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ need a maximum of 13 points to regain Victory League title after getting all five points at Mevagissey ‘B’.

more They were made to fight hard as the first three frames all went down to the black but Knight, Pearson, Bilson, Cocks and McIntosh are clear favourites for the silverware. Paul Williams knocked in an excellent 56 break to beat league chairman Garry Stephens, but that was Mevagissey ‘A’s only success as Bethel ‘A’ went on to win through Matt Green, Leon Collard, Andy Peers and Geoff Hawken (walkover). Bugle ‘B’ and Pelynt had a right ding-dong battle as Ian Powell and Dave Rundle put the home side 2-0 up. But the visitors pulled one back with Len Flower’s great win over Mark Sturtridge. Colin Rundle won it for Bugle beating Richard Broad with Phil Ormond winning a close final frame against Keith Moore for the second away point. Chris Morford and Nick Kendall, who knocked in a 31 break, put St Blazey 2-0 up at Tregonissey ‘B’ who fought back with wins for Mike Hooley and Andrew Gooch with Dean Willis beating Justin Hooley in the last to clinch victory for the Blazey boys. Mevagissey ‘D’ and St Neot has been rescheduled for next week. There isn’t much movement in the Rees League as Bethel ‘C’s game at Tregonissey ‘C’ was postponed but Bethel ‘B’ moved joint third after beating Bugle ‘C’s 4-1. Roy Bayliss got the visitors point beating Martin Isted but wins for Guy Buckle, Jim Tamblyn, Richie Bolt and Clive Stuthridge who was pushed all the way by Damian Knight did the job for the home side. Bethel ‘D’ picked up three points at St Stephen. Good wins for Gavin Dyche, Steve Benallack and Jason Pascoe put them in a strong position only for the home side to take the final two frames with black-ball wins for Rob Garside and Mark Sullivan against Dave Morshead and Ross McMenemy respectively. Lostwithiel picked up a good 3-2 home win over Gorran, Mike McGurrell and Darren Biggen’s black-ball victory over Geoff Hayhurst put them 2-0 up, with Steve Williams beating Terry Nicholls to pull one back. Craig Nicholls sealed the win getting the better of Andy Reddall with Lee McKinstry getting Gorran second point beating Josh Doherty.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bethel ‘A’ 4 (M Mills, L Collard, A Peers, G Hawken) Mevagissey ‘A’ 1 (P Williams (56)), Mevagisey ‘B’ 0 Tregonissey ‘A’ 5 (M Knight, R Pearson, R Bilson, J Cocks, B McIntosh), Bugle ‘B’ 3 (I Powell, D Rundle, C Rundle) Pelynt 2 (L Flower, P Ormond), Tregonissey ‘B’ 2 (M Hooley, A Gooch) St Blazey 3 (C Morford, N Kendall (31), D Willis). Rees Memorial League: Lostwithiel 3 (M McGurral, D Biggen, C Nicholls) Gorran 2 (S Williams, L McKinstry), Bethel ‘B’ 4 (G Buckle, J Tamblyn, R Bolt, C Stuthridge) Bugle ‘C’ 1 (R Bayliss), St Stephen 2 (R Garside, M Sullivan) Bethel ‘D’ 3 (G Dyche, S Benallack, J Pascoe). Top breaks of the week: N Kendall 31, P Williams 56 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Tregonissey increase their lead at the top', 'tregonissey-increase-their-lead-at-the-top', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ increased their lead at the top of the St Blazey and District Snooker League Victory League after a 4-1 win at Lostwithiel ‘A’ also in the process capturing the possession of the Runabout Shield.', null, '"Bethel ‘C’ suffered a damaging 4-1 home reverse to Bugle ‘B’, Courtney Crockford won the last frame of the evening against Keith Moore but wins for Dave Rundle, Mark Sturtridge, Ian Ball and Jake Moore, who had a 31, took the points for the Bugle boys."', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ increased their lead at the top of the St Blazey and District Snooker League Victory League after a 4-1 win at Lostwithiel ‘A’ also in the process capturing the possession of the Runabout Shield.

more Chris Perring’s win over Martin Knight was the home side’s only success with Barry McIntosh knocking in a 45 in his victory over Steve Courts. Bugle ‘A’ slipped to their seventh defeat of the season at home to Mevagissey ‘A’, who strengthened their grip on second place. Paul Williams knocked in an excellent 47 break during his victory over Gary Spencer and there were also wins for Chris Kendall and Matt Mills with Andrew Best and Paul Sturtridge getting the home team’s points. Bethel ‘A’ returned from Mevagissey ‘B’ with four points getting the better of two very close frames. Tonkin, Stephens, Hawken and Collard victorious with Mark Gregory on target for the home side. Bethel ‘C’ suffered a damaging 4-1 home reverse to Bugle ‘B’, Courtney Crockford won the last frame of the evening against Keith Moore but wins for Dave Rundle, Mark Sturtridge, Ian Ball and Jake Moore, who had a 31, took the points for the Bugle boys. In the Rees League Lostwithiel ‘B’s relentless title surge continued at basement boys St Stephen ‘B’ where they took all five frames with Lenny Floer knocking in a 34 break. All below them dropped points but Bethel ‘D’ look favourites for the runners-up spot after beating Mevagissey ‘C’ 4-1. Ben Stunnel, Ryan Tonkin, Steve Benallack and Andy Spearman, who pipped ‘the predator’ Tregale were their winners with Joe Chesterfield seeing off Jason Pascoe for the home point. Mevagissey ‘D’ made the short trip to Gorran Haven and won 3-2. Richard Holmes and Lee McKinstry, who had an excellent victory over James Tomlison, were on target for the Haven. Tregonissey ‘B’ continued their fine season seeing off St Stephen ‘A’ 3-2. Ryan Thomas, Chris Robins and Phil Hooley were on target with Dan Allen and Anthony Harrison replying. Bugle ‘C’ scored two points through Nigel Couchman and Kevin Spencer, where they travelled to Bethel ‘B’ who replied through Rich Allen, Dave Morshead and Jim Tamblyn.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bethel ‘C’ 1 (C Crockford) Bugle ‘B’ 4 (D Rundle, M Sturtridge, I Ball, J Moore (31)) Bugle ‘A’ 2 (A Best, P Sturtridge) Mevagissey ‘A’ 3 (C Kendall, P Williams (47), M Mills), Lostwithiel ‘A’ 1 (C Perring) Tregonissey ‘A’ 4 (B McIntosh (45), R Bilson, C Stephenson, J Cocks), Mevagissey ‘B’ 1 (M Gregory) Bethel ‘A’ 4 (R Tonkin, G Stephens, G Hawken, L Collard). Rees Memorial League: Tregonissey ‘B’ 3 (R Thomas, C Robins, P Hooley) St Stephen ‘A’ 2 (D Allen, A Harrison), Bethel ‘B’ 3 (R Allen, D Morshead, J Tamblyn) Bugle ‘C’ 2 (N Couchman, K Spencer), St Stephen ‘B’ 0 Lostwithiel 5 (G Lory, A Parnell, J Daniell, R Broad, L Flower), Gorran Haven 2 Mevagissey 3, Mevagissey ‘C’ 1 (J Chesterfield) Bethel ‘D’ 4 (B Stunnell, R Tonkin, A Spearman, S Benallack). Top breaks of the week: J Moore 31, P Williams 47, B McIntosh 45 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Tregonissey lead the way by seven points', 'tregonissey-lead-the-way-by-seven-points', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ have opened up a seven-point lead at the top of the St Blazey and District Victory League with six matches to go after beating Mevagissey ‘B’ 5-0 and are clear favourites for the title.', null, '"In the Rees League Bethel ‘D’ are clear favourites to be runners-up after beating Tregonissey ‘B’ 3-2. Jason Pascoe, Steve Benallack and Andy Spearman’s black-ball win over Justin Hooley took the the points with Phil Hooley and Chris Robins both playing excellently to record the away points."', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ have opened up a seven-point lead at the top of the St Blazey and District Victory League with six matches to go after beating Mevagissey ‘B’ 5-0 and are clear favourites for the title.

more Mevagissey ‘A’ have moved six points clear of Bugle ‘A’ and St Blazey ‘B’ after seeing off Lostwithiel ‘A’ 3-2. Pete Chambers, Paul Williams, with a 32 break, and captain Phil Staley, with a 35 were their winners, with Darren Lock and Steve Courts replying. Bugle ‘B’ picked up an excellent 3-2 home victory over St Blazey ‘B’ – Ian Ball, Mark Sturtridge and Colin Rundle who was making a rare appearance were on target. Paul Baron and Mike Hancock who recorded a very tasty 69 break replied for the Blazey boys. Whilst basement boys Bethel ‘C’ had a poor night losing 5-0 to St Blazey ‘A’ who have cut the gap between themselves and Lostwithiel ‘A’ to five points. In the Rees League Bethel ‘D’ are clear favourites to be runners-up after beating Tregonissey ‘B’ 3-2. Jason Pascoe, Steve Benallack and Andy Spearman’s black-ball win over Justin Hooley took the the points with Phil Hooley and Chris Robins both playing excellently to record the away points. Richard Holmes continued his recent good form beating Richard Broad as Gorran Haven went down 4-1 at champions elect Lostwithiel ‘B’. Mevagissey ‘C’ are in free-fall – they led 2-0 at Lerryn through Charlie Prynn and Joe Chesterfield but the home side replied through Graham Hoskin, Gordon Brimicombe and Terry Middleton to seal the win. St Stephen ‘A’ and Bethel’B’ were level at two each when Anthony Harrison knocked in a fine 31 break against Mike Sweet to seal the victory for the home side. Bugle ‘C’ picked up their sixth win of the season beating St Stephen ‘B’ 4-1. Kevin Spencer, Ed Cooke, Dave Knight and Sam Wingrave the winners.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: St Blazey ‘A’ 5 (M Meares, N Kendall, C Brown, L Roseveare, S Dewing) Bethel ‘C’ 0, Bugle ‘B’ 3 (I Ball, C Rundle, M Sturtridge) St Blazey ‘B’ 2 (M Hancock (69), P Baron), Mevagissey ‘A’ 3 (P Chambers, P Williams (32), P Staley (35) Lostwithiel ‘A’ 2 (S Courts, D Lock), Tregonissey ‘A’ 5 (B McIntosh, R Bilson, C Stephenson, J Cocks, M Knight) Mevagissey ‘B’ 0. Rees Memorial League: St Stephen ‘A’ 3 (C Clark, A Garside, A Harrison (31), Bethel ‘B’ 2 (C Sturtridge, D Moorshead), Bugle ‘C’ 4 (K Spencer, S Wingrave, E Cooke, D Knight) St Stephen ‘B’ 1 (R Garside), Lostwithiel ‘B’ 4 (A Parnall, L Flower, J Daniell, G Lory) Gorran Haven 1 (R Holmes), Lerryn 3 (G Hoskin, G Brimicombe, T Middleton) Mevagissey ‘C’ 2 (C Prynn, J Chesterfield), Bethel ‘D’ 3 (J Pascoe, A Spearman, S benallack) Tregonissey ‘B’ 2 (P Hooley, C Robins). Top breaks of the week: M Hancock 69, P Williams 32, P Staley 35, A Harrison 31 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Tregonissey put their feet up and reap the rewards', 'tregonissey-put-their-feet-up-and-reap-the-rewards', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'St Blazey and District Snooker League leaders Tregonissey ‘A’ enjoyed a good night on their bye week as the top three chasing teams all dropped valuable points.', null, '"Lostwithiel ‘B’s relentless title charge continued beating St Stephen ‘A’ 4-1 although four black-ball frames on the night suggested things could have been a lot different. Dan Allen produced a good win over John Daniell but it was scant reward for St Stephen’s efforts on the night."', 'St Blazey and District Snooker League leaders Tregonissey ‘A’ enjoyed a good night on their bye week as the top three chasing teams all dropped valuable points.

more Second-placed Mevagissey ‘A’ lost at home to Bethel ‘A’ despite a fine performance from Chris Kendall and Paul Williams, who weighed in with a 35 but in the final frame Leon Collard was already 20 up on out-of-sorts Pete Chambers when he potted a 32 break to take the win for Bethel. While in the Bugle derby the ‘B’ team gained the Runabout Shield by defeating their ‘A’ team 3-2. Mark Sturtridge, Jake Moore who had a 33 break, and Keith Moore’s excellent victory over Gary Spencer secured the win to make sure the shield went over to the other side on the table. St Blazey ‘B’ maintained their outside chance of silverware after returning from Lostwithiel ‘A’ with a 3-2 win. Mike Hancock’s 33 and wins from Paul Baron and Graham Taylor were enough to claim the points with Darren Lock and Cyril Dickenson replying. Bethel ‘C’ won the battle of the bottom two as Martin Powell, Howard Brett and the in-form Paul Thomas were their winners with Dave Kendall and Ben Rothwell on target for Mevagissey ‘B’. Lostwithiel ‘B’s relentless title charge continued beating St Stephen ‘A’ 4-1 although four black-ball frames on the night suggested things could have been a lot different. Dan Allen produced a good win over John Daniell but it was scant reward for St Stephen’s efforts on the night. There are now four teams separated by only two points with Bethel ‘B’s 3-2 home victory over Mevagissey ‘C’ meaning they join the opponents in joint second place. Dave Morshead, Guy Buckle and Clive Stuthridge gained victories with Ben and Charlie Prynn replying. Rob Blamey won the final frame for Mevagissey ‘D’ but it was their only success as Ben Stunnell, Shaun Hicks, Andy Spearman and Ryan Tonkin showed a return to winning ways for Bethel ‘D’. Bugle ‘C’ made the long trip to Gorran Haven and returned with a 4-1 victory courtesy of Sam Wingrave, Dave Knight, Kevin Spencer and Nigel Couchman with Lee McKinstry getting Gorran’s point. Tregonissey ‘B’ pipped Lerryn 3-2. Chris Robins, Justin Hooley and Sam Simon won for Treggy with Graham Hoskin and Gordon Brimicombe replying.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bugle ‘A’ 2 (P Sturtridge, I Powell) Bugle ‘B’ 3 (M Sturtridge, J Moore (33), K Moore), Mevagissey ‘A’ 2 (C Kendall, P Williams (35) Bethel ‘A’ 3 (S Allen, G Hawken, L Collard (32), Lostwithiel ‘A’ 2 (D Lock, C Dickenson) St Blazey ‘B’ 3 (P Baron, M Hancock (33), G Taylor) Bethel ‘C’ 3 (M Powell, H Brett, P Thomas) Mevagissey ‘B’ 2 (D Kendall, B Rothwell). Rees Memorial League: St Stephen ‘A’ 1 (D Allen) Lostwithiel ‘B’ 4 (B Warne, G Lory, R Broad, L Flower), Gorran Haven 1 (L McKinstry) Bugle ‘C’ 4 (D Knight, S Wingrave, N Couchman, K Spencer), Mevagissey ‘D’ 1 (R Blamey) Bethel ‘D’ 4 (B Stunnel, S Hicks, R Tonkin, A Spearman), Tregonissey ‘B’ 3 (C Robins, J Hooley, S Simon), Lerryn 2 (G Brimicombe, G Hoskin), Bethel ‘B’ 3 (D Morshead, G Buckle, C Stuthridge) Mevagissey ‘C’ 2 (B Prynn, C Prynn). Top breaks of the week: J Moore 33, P Williams 35, L Collard 32, M Hancock 33 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Tregonissey switch their game and are made to pay the price', 'tregonissey-switch-their-game-and-are-made-to-pay-the-price', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Leaders Tregonissey ‘A’ switched their game to Mevagissey ‘A’ after problems with their own table and will be regretting it as Meva romped to a 4-1 victory to move into second place.', null, '"Bethel ‘B’ trounced Bethel ‘D’ 4-1 which drops the ‘D’ team down to fifth place somewhat surprising after such a great start to the season only Ben Stunnell could win as Guy Buckle, Jim Tamblyn, Clive Stuthridge and Rich Allen prevailed."', 'Leaders Tregonissey ‘A’ switched their game to Mevagissey ‘A’ after problems with their own table and will be regretting it as Meva romped to a 4-1 victory to move into second place.

more Barry McIntosh was Treggy’s only winner as Paul Williams’ 39 break inflicted a first defeat of the season on Ryan Bilson, followed by wins for Chris Kendall, Paul Chambers and Phil Staley. St Blazey ‘B’ saw off defending champions Bugle ‘A’ 3-2, giving Bugle their fourth defeat in the opening 10 games. Mike Hancock continued his good form registering a 50 break against Ian Powell while Gary Spencer had a 34 when seeing off Graham Taylor. Bugle ‘B’ picked up a handy 3-2 home win over St Blazey ‘A’ – Nick Kendall’s 30 the highlight of the away team’s night as Jake Moore, Ian Ball and Dave Rundle won for the hosts. Bethel ‘C’ were unlucky to lose 3-2 to their ‘A’ side, Paul Tomas and Howard Brett won their frames but were beaten by victories for Jason Pascoe, Steve Allen and Leon Courts. With leaders Lostwithiel ‘B’ on their bye week none of the chasing pack took real advantage, Mevagissey ‘C’ lost at home to Tregonissey ‘B’ with Matt Chesterfield and predator Tregale getting victories but were outdone by Phil Hooley, Ryan Thomas and Sam Simon. Bethel ‘B’ trounced Bethel ‘D’ 4-1 which drops the ‘D’ team down to fifth place somewhat surprising after such a great start to the season only Ben Stunnell could win as Guy Buckle, Jim Tamblyn, Clive Stuthridge and Rich Allen prevailed. Mevagissey ‘D’ got another win this time at Lerryn to close the gap on the chasing pack. Gordon Brimicombe saw off Neil Parker but Rich Lakeman, Rob Blamey, Scott Mills and James Tomlinson won for the away side. St Stephen ‘A’ won at Bugle ‘C’ through Garside, Clark and Olson with Mike Hodge and Nigel Couchman potting the home points. And finally at the 11th time of the asking St Stephen ‘B’ won their first match by beating Gorran Haven 3-2. Jon Rosevear, Brad Christian and Rob Garside were the heroes for the basement boys.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bugle ‘B’ 3 (J Moore, I Ball, D Rundle) St Blazey ‘A’ 2 (C Brown, N Kendall), Tregonissey ‘A’ 1 (B McIntosh) Mevagissey ‘A’ 4 (P Chambers, P Williams (39), C Kendall, P Staley), Bethel ‘A’ 3 (L Collard, S Allen, J Pascoe) Bethel ‘C’ 2 (P Thomas, H Brett), St Blazey ‘B’ 3 (M Hancock (50), P Baron, B Phillips) Bugle ‘A’ 2 (G Spencer (34), A Peers). Rees Memorial League: Bugle ‘C’ 2 (M Hodge, N Couchman) St Stephen ‘A’ 3 (A Garside, C Clark, J Olson), Lerryn 1 (G Brimicombe) Mevagissey ‘D’ 4 (R Blamey, R Lakeman, S Mills, J Tomlinson), Bethel ‘D’ 1 (B Stunnell) Bethel ‘B’ 4 (G Buckle, J Tamblyn, C Stutridge, R Allen), St Stephen ‘B’ 3 (B Christian, J Rosevear, R Garside) Gorran Haven 2 (B Cooling, R Holmes), Mevagissey ‘C’ 2 (M Chesterfield, D Tregale) Tregonisey ‘B’ 3 (P Hooley, R Thomas, S Simon). Top breaks of the week: M Hancock 50, G Spencer 34, P Williams 39 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Tregonissey take over the top after 5-0 victory', 'tregonissey-take-over-the-top-after-5-0-victory', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'It’s all change at the top of both St Blazey and District Snooker Leagues this week with some interesting scores along the way.', null, '"Bethel ‘A’ were glad to crawl over the line for victory over Mevagissey ‘B’ – fit again Leon Collard and Matt Green put the home side two up but Matt Chesterfield and Mark Gregory restored parity and it was left for Steve Allen’s black ball win over Nathan Shaw to secure victory."', 'It’s all change at the top of both St Blazey and District Snooker Leagues this week with some interesting scores along the way.

more Tregonissey ‘A’ scored a fantastic 5-0 win over Lostwithiel ‘A’ to reach the summit. Barry McIntosh scoring a 36 break to win over Steve Courts and Jason Cocks victory over Darren Lock were crucial as they look now to stay on the summit for as long as possible in a very tight league. Mevagissey ‘A’ moved into second place after beating previous leaders Bugle ‘A’ 4-1 Matt Mills and Pete Chambers put them 2-0 up but Ian Powell pulled one back beating Chris Kendall only for Phil Staley to inflict Gary Spencer’s first defeat of the season before in the final frame Andrew Best was well in front but Paul Williams put in a fine 33 break to clinch a tremendous victory. Bethel ‘A’ were glad to crawl over the line for victory over Mevagissey ‘B’ – fit again Leon Collard and Matt Green put the home side two up but Matt Chesterfield and Mark Gregory restored parity and it was left for Steve Allen’s black ball win over Nathan Shaw to secure victory. Whilst Bethel ‘C’ felt aggrieved to come away from Bugle ‘B’ with a defeat as Martin Powell and Courtney Crockford, who recorded his first win of the season, came up short. Jake Moore with the help of a 33 break against Paul Thomas helped secure the win. Lostwithiel ‘B’s 5-0 win over St Stephen ‘B’ saw them leap to the top of the Rees League the newcomers got close in a couple of frames but they were outdone by a more experienced opposition. Bethel ‘D’ were very pleased to beat Mevagissey ‘C’ 4-1 and maintain second place. Matt Chesterfield again played well on the night for the visitors to see off Steve Benallack. Tregonissey ‘B’ crashed 4-1 at St Stephen ‘A’, Justin Hooley played well to see off Stacey Hancock but Garside, Bennett, Harrison and Clark saw the home side win. Some very tight frames were played out at Mevagissey as the ‘D’ team beat Gorran Haven 3-2 to record their third straight win. Lee McKinstry and Rob Bunney notched for the visitors who are having a much-improved season. Bethel ‘B’ came home from Bugle ‘C’ with a 3-2 victory. Kev Spencer did better than son Gary by beating Guy Buckle and Sam Wingrave beat Mike Sweet but a black ball victory for Dave Morshead in the decider against Dave Knight saw the Bethel boys home.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bugle ‘B’ 3 (I Ball, K Moore, J Moore (33)) Bethel ‘C’ 2 (M Powell, C Crockford), Mevagissey ‘A’ 4 (P Staley, M Mills, P Williams (33), P Chambers) Bugle ‘A’ 1 (I Powell), Tregonissey ‘A’ 5 (R Bilson, B McIntosh (36), J Cocks, C Stephenson, M Knight) Lostwithiel ‘A’ 0, Bethel ‘A’ 3 (L Collard, M Green, S Allen) Mevagissey ‘B’ 2 (M Chesterfield, M Gregory). Rees Memorial League: St Stephen ‘A’ 4 (A Garside, J Bennett, A Harrison, C Clark) Tregonissey ‘B’ 1 (J Hooley), Bugle ‘C’ 2 (K Spencer, S Wingrave) Bethel ‘B’ 3 (J Tamblyn, R Allen, D Moorshead), Lostwithiel ‘B’ 5 (G Lory, B Warne, R Broad, A Parnell, L Flower) St Stephen 0, Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (R Lakeman, J Tomlinson, N Parker) Gorran Haven 2 (R Bunney, L McKinstry), Bethel ‘D’ 4 (R Tonkin, A Spearman, S Hicks, J Pascoe) Mevagissey ‘C’ 1 (M Chesterfield). Top breaks of the week: J Moore 33, P Williams 33, B McIntosh 36 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Tregonissey win back the Runabout Shield', 'tregonissey-win-back-the-runabout-shield', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'A night of high class snooker saw Tregonissey ‘A’ regain the Runabout Shield after demolishing previously unbeaten Bugle ‘A’ to go equal top with Bethel ‘A’.', null, 'Richard Opie equalled the Rees League’s highest break of the season of 40 as he helped Tregonissey ‘B’ to stay clear at the top after defeating Lerryn 4-1. Charles McMiram’s good run continued after a shaky start to the season when defeating Ryan Thomas but Justin, Phil and Mike Hooley along with Richard saw Treggy home.', 'A night of high class snooker saw Tregonissey ‘A’ regain the Runabout Shield after demolishing previously unbeaten Bugle ‘A’ to go equal top with Bethel ‘A’.

more Ryan Bilson’s 32 whilst beating Ian Ball got the ball rolling following by Matt Woodward’s win over Paul Sturtridge. Jason cocks knocked in a 50 break against Gary Spencer but they were to be his only points scored as Gary responded with a 30 break himself to claim the home point. Jason Simmonds knocked in a 54 when thrashing Andrew Best and with Martin Knight hammering Jake Moore it really was a bad night for the home side. Bethel ‘A’ join Treggy at the summit after a very tight 3-2 win over Bugle ‘B’ who slump to the bottom after this defeat. Andy Peers and Matt Green won the first two after beating Tony Conroy and Keith Moore. Bugle evened things up after Dave and Colin Rundle both played well to see off Geoff Hawken and Leon Collard respectively and it was long into the night when Gary Stephens finally beat Mark Sturtridge to finally seal the win. St Stephen ‘A’ finally got their first win with an excellent 3-2 victory at St Blazey ‘A’. Anthony Harrison’s great win over Nick Kendall. Adrian Bowyer (Mark Craddock) and Anthony Garside (Mick Meares) were their heroes with Aiden Maxwell Craddock and Stewart Dewings replying. Pelynt pick up a fine 3-2 away win at Mevagissey ‘B’ to edge a little but closer to safety. John Daniell, Richard Broad and Phil Ormand did the damage with Dave Kendall and Matt Rowe replying. Richard Opie equalled the Rees League’s highest break of the season of 40 as he helped Tregonissey ‘B’ to stay clear at the top after defeating Lerryn 4-1. Charles McMiram’s good run continued after a shaky start to the season when defeating Ryan Thomas but Justin, Phil and Mike Hooley along with Richard saw Treggy home. Bethel ‘B’ jumped to second after a good 4-1 win at Gorran. Matt Fix opened Bethel’s account by beating Martin Isted but comfortable wins for Jim Tamblyn, Clive Stuthridge, Guy Buckle and Richard Bolt saw the title contenders home. Bethel ‘D’ keep edging closer to the top after a good win at Mevagissey ‘C’. They opened up a two-frame lead through Jason Pascoe and Ross McMenemy but Joe Chesterfield beat Andy Spearman to start a comeback but with Ryan Tonkin seeing off Predator Tregale Bethel’s win was assured. Ben Prynn got Meva’s second point by beating Lee Searle. Howard Brett’s 34 clearance helped him give Rich Lakeman his first defeat of the season by Meva ‘D’ recovered to inflict a 3-2 defeat on Bethel ‘C’. Mark Ashton, Ian Latham and George Lory secured win for the away side with Ben Stunnell replying for Bethel.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bugle ‘A’ 1 (G Spencer (30)) Tregonissey ‘A’ 4 (R Bilson (32), J Simmonds (54), M Woodward, M Knight), St Blazey ‘A’ 2 (A Maxwell Craddock, S Dewings) St Stephen ‘A’ 3 (A Harrison, A Bowyer, A Garside), Mevagissey ‘B’ 2 (D Kendall, M Rowe) Pelynt 3 (J Daniell, R Broad, P Ormand), Bethel ‘A’ 3 (A Peers, M Green, G Stephens) Bugle ‘B’ 2 (D Rundle, C Rundle). Rees Memorial League: Bethel ‘C’ 2 (H Brett, B Stunnell) Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (I Latham, M Ashton, G Lory), Gorran 1 (M Fox) Bethel ‘B’ 4 (J Tamblyn, C Stuthridge, G Buckle, R Bolt), Mevagissey ‘C’ 2 (J Chesterfield, B Prynn) Bethel ‘D’ 3 (J Pascoe, R McMenemy, R Tonkin), Tregonissey ‘B’ 4 (J Hooley, P Hooley, R Opie (40), M Hooley) Lerryn 1 (C McMiram). Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: G Spencer 30, R Bilson 32, J Simmonds 54, R Opie 40, H Brett 34 Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Trump Brings Down Rocket', 'trump-brings-down-rocket', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Judd Trump won six frames in a row to beat Ronnie O''Sullivan 10-7 in the final of the inaugural 888.com World Grand Prix.', null, '"I felt comfortable out there. I took confidence from making the 142 to go 7-7, and after that I didn''t really miss a lot. I missed the blue in the last frame, but other than that I felt I dominated tonight. My safety was very good and I think that was the difference. I''m the sort of player who gets on a roll as soon as I win one or two frames."', 'Judd Trump won six frames in a row to beat Ronnie O''Sullivan 10-7 in the final of the inaugural 888.com World Grand Prix.

more Just as he did in the final of the World Championship last season against Mark Selby, O''Sullivan lost his way in the closing stages and let slip a sizeable lead. Instead it was 25-year-old Trump who came from 7-4 down to soar to the trophy and £100,000 top prize at this new tournament in Llandudno. It''s Trump''s second title of the season and first since the Australian Goldfields Open in July. More significantly for the Bristolian left-hander, he beat O''Sullivan in a major final, and a match over more than one session, for the first time. O''Sullivan had come out on top when they clashed in the Champion of Champions and UK Championship before Christmas, but this time it was the Ace who held the winning hand. There''s barely time to celebrate tonight''s victory as world number seven Trump flies to Thailand tomorrow for the Players Championship and then heads to Beijing for the China Open. Imbued with the confidence that he can beat the best on the big stage, Trump will have no fear as he goes into those events and the next domestic tournament: the World Championship. An exciting final brought to an end a superb week of snooker at Venue Cymru in North Wales. Packed crowds, dramatic matches and a superb location have made this a fine addition to the calendar. Leading 5-4 after the first session, five-time World Champion O''Sullivan extended his lead in the opening frame tonight with a break of 96. In frame 11 he enjoyed a huge slice of luck, fluking a red to a baulk corner to set up a run of 59 which made it 7-4. World number three O''Sullivan had first chance in frame 12, but missed a black off its spot on 29. Trump made 57 before missing the penultimate red, but he got another opportunity and clawed back to 7-5. Trump dominated the next as well with runs of 47 and 25 to trail by just one frame at the mid-session interval. Trump continued to gain momentum in frame 14 with a stunning 142 total clearance, the highest break of the tournament, worth a handy £5,000 bonus. In frame 15, O''Sullivan was on 28 when he mis-cued, sending the white ball sailing onto the carpet. Trump built a 60-35 lead and clinched the frame after laying a snooker on the last red, to take the lead for the first time since 1-0. Both players passed up chances in the 16th, with O''Sullivan''s shot selection reckless at times, and it came down to a safety tussle on the brown. Trump was partially snooker behind the blue and studied a possible long pot for over two minutes, before crashing it in and clearing the table to go 9-7 ahead. In the next, Trump made 36 before missing the blue to centre, then O''Sullivan''s response faltered on just nine. Trump knocked in a long red and added 29 which proved enough for the title. "Not a lot of people come back from that far behind against Ronnie so I''m over the moon," said Trump. "I''ve dug in all week. I didn''t play great stuff, nowhere near my best. At 4-1 down, I was getting a little bit frustrated and then it seemed to turn. It was a weird game, a bit patchy. In the end I kept calm and got over the line. "It''s a great feeling to beat Ronnie in a big final for the first time. You don''t want to lose too many times in a row against the same player. Two times is already a lot, and going behind tonight 7-4, I thought it was just going to be the same thing. "I felt comfortable out there. I took confidence from making the 142 to go 7-7, and after that I didn''t really miss a lot. I missed the blue in the last frame, but other than that I felt I dominated tonight. My safety was very good and I think that was the difference. I''m the sort of player who gets on a roll as soon as I win one or two frames. "I won the tournament playing a different game to what I''m used to. My safety was probably a lot better than anyone else''s throughout the whole tournament, and that got me through. If I''m not potting every ball on the table, I need to change the game. "The venue''s been excellent all week. The crowd has been brilliant and I look forward to coming back next year. "The money will go towards some holidays in the summer, but there''s still a lot of money to be won during the rest of this season." O''Sullivan, who generously gave his silver runner-up medal to a disabled man in the crowd called Steffan Campbell, said: "I''m disappointed to have lost but I''m proud of my achievements this week. "Judd''s a bit like Selby. If you go three, four, five frames up, they seem to play better when they''re behind. I expected him to come back at me. I wasn''t playing well enough to be able to keep my foot to the pedal. I knew I was going to give him chances. Some days you''re not timing the ball well, and today was one of those days. "Hopefully this tournament is here to stay. It''s a great venue and a great tournament. It''s a proper event, you really appreciate the quality of it." Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: Tai Chengzhe', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Trump Strikes Gold', 'trump-strikes-gold', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Judd Trump won his first world ranking title in 20 months by beating Neil Robertson 9-5 in the final of the Australian Goldfields Open.', null, '"Last season I didn''t play well at the start but I did in the second half and it was frustrating not to win a title. It''s great to get off to a good start this season and hopefully I can win more. "', 'Judd Trump won his first world ranking title in 20 months by beating Neil Robertson 9-5 in the final of the Australian Goldfields Open.

more Bristol''s 24-year-old Trump won the fourth ranking title of his career by out-playing home favourite Robertson in Bendigo. It''s Trump''s first ranking trophy since the 2012 International Championship in China - when he beat Robertson in the final. Melbourne-born Robertson had beaten Trump 13-11 in the quarter-finals of the World Championship two months ago, but this time he missed out on the chance to win his home tournament for the first time. For the second year in a row the 32-year-old had to settle for the runner-up prize, having lost in the final to Marco Fu 12 months ago. He had also been bidding to win back-to-back ranking titles having won last week''s Wuxi Classic. However Robertson can take consolation from the fact that he has regained the world number one ranking from Mark Selby. Trump himself stood at the top of the world rankings following his 2012 International Championship victory. He subsequently suffered a dip in form and slipped out of the top four. The left-hander showed signs of his best towards the end of last season, particularly in reaching the German Masters final in February. And now the talented former UK Champion has broken his drought and flies home with a piece of silverware as well as a cheque for $75,000. Today''s final saw the first six frames shared, Trump making the bigger breaks with 50 and 71. The Englishman, who last played in this tournament in 2011, then made a decisive surge. Runs of 55 and 114 gave him the last two frames of the afternoon session, then in the first frame of the concluding session he cleared from blue to black to go 6-3 up. Trump took frame ten in three scoring visits then made a 101, his eighth century of the tournament, to lead 8-3. Robertson battled gamely, dominating frame 12 then winning the next with a break of 109 to close to 8-5. He had chances in the next but could not take advantage and Trump eventually potted the final blue to seal the match. "I played well throughout the week although today was quite cagey," said Trump. "Even at 8-3 ahead I knew he was capable of winning six frames in a row - he did that against me at the World Championship. He started to go for his shots and they were going in, so I was a bit nervy because if it had gone 8-6 then the pressure would have been on me. "The difference today was that I was nicking the tight frames. I was happy to be 5-3 ahead at the end of the first session, then I won an important first frame in the evening session. "Last season I didn''t play well at the start but I did in the second half and it was frustrating not to win a title. It''s great to get off to a good start this season and hopefully I can win more. "I''m glad I entered the event, I enjoyed every game and the crowds were a lot better than the first time I came here." Robertson said: "This week I had a pretty comfortable path to the final, but Judd definitely played the best snooker and he was just too good for me today. I want to congratulate him and thank him for entering the tournament this year. I hope he''s enjoyed it as the playing conditions are the best we play on all year, and the crowd have been fantastic as well. "We can both play a lot better than we did today, we kept making 30 or 40 then running out of position. He capitalised a lot better than I did, otherwise instead of 8-3 it could have been 6-5 either way. Let''s hope next year can be third time lucky for me." Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: Tai Chengzhe', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Trump conquers China', 'trump-conquers-china', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Judd Trump won the BAIC Motor China Open for the second time with an emphatic 10-4 victory over Ricky Walden in the final.', null, '“I didn’t play well at the start of the match, but from 3-1 down I played better and built a good lead. Ricky struggled today and my safety was better than his. I didn’t play as well as I did earlier in the week but it’s a good result.', 'Judd Trump won the BAIC Motor China Open for the second time with an emphatic 10-4 victory over Ricky Walden in the final.

more Trump landed the fifth ranking title of his career and first since the 2014 Australian Goldfields Open, 21 months ago, by dominating the final in Beijing. The tournament which gave the Bristolian his first ranking title five years ago once again yielded the trophy as well as a cheque for £85,000. World number seven Trump, age 26, played superb snooker throughout the tournament, winning 36 frames and conceding just nine in six matches. It’s his third ranking title in China, having also won the International Championship in 2012, and makes amends for a shock 10-9 defeat against Kyren Wilson at the Shanghai Masters in September. Left-hander Trump will now go to the World Championship later this month full of confidence, especially having reached the final at the Crucible a few weeks after his first China Open triumph in 2011. Chester’s Walden suffered his second defeat in a ranking event final within a week, having lost 10-6 to Mark Allen at the Players Championship last Sunday. Indeed today’s contest followed a similar pattern as he led 3-1 early on but then lost control. The world number ten banks £35,000 but will be disappointed to have lost a third consecutive ranking final and missed out on a fourth title, the previous three having all come in China. Walden took the opening frame with a break of 71 and cleared from blue to black in the second for 2-0. Trump won a scrappy third before Walden’s 108 made it 3-1. Trump took the fifth with a run of 90 and from that point he dominated, winning a succession of frames on the colours as he went 8-3 ahead. A run of 69 helped extend Trump’s lead, and although Walden pulled one back with a 64 to make it 9-4, there would be no fight back as Trump sealed victory in the next with a 55. “I’m very happy to win this again,” said Trump. “It’s the first time I have won a tournament twice and it gives me a lot of confidence. “I didn’t play well at the start of the match, but from 3-1 down I played better and built a good lead. Ricky struggled today and my safety was better than his. I didn’t play as well as I did earlier in the week but it’s a good result. “Every time I come here I try my hardest. I started well in my first match this week and then kept it up. I was never really in trouble, the hardest game was against Mark King in the quarter-finals when I came from 3-2 down to win 5-3. Otherwise I didn’t drop many frames. “At 9-3 I lost my concentration a bit, but that helped me to get my focus back and I made a good break in the last frame.” Walden said: “It was a difficult game. I didn’t play well all day, I missed too many balls and made too many mistakes. Judd deserved to win and it’s a tough one to take for me, although it’s still a decent week to get to the final. “I started off strong and then from 3-3 I lost momentum very quickly. I lost concentration and lost my way. I was trying my hardest to stick in there but I lost a lot of close frames. It was just one of those days where things went wrong. “The top players are all very similar but on the day you have to handle it, and Judd handled it better than me today. The game is all about lifting trophies and I haven’t managed to do that, but I have played some good stuff in the last two weeks. It hurts to lose but I’ll take the positives and I’ll be back stronger.” Article & Images courtesy of the World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Trump defends European Masters crown', 'trump-defends-european-masters-crown', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Judd Trump has defended a ranking title for the first time in his career, winning the 888sport European Masters thanks to a thrilling 9-7 defeat of Stuart Bingham in Lommel.', null, '“Winning this tournament last year gave me a good incentive to defend the title. Now I have got that off my back I can set myself new targets for the rest of the season.”', 'Judd Trump has defended a ranking title for the first time in his career, winning the 888sport European Masters thanks to a thrilling 9-7 defeat of Stuart Bingham in Lommel.

more Trump recorded a stunning 9-8 victory over Ronnie O’Sullivan to lift the title a year ago. 12 months later and the Ace in the Pack found himself in the final once again. He faced a repeat of the Welsh Open final from earlier in the year, which he narrowly lost to Bingham. On this occasion it was the 28-year-old Trump who came out on top. Victory and the £75,000 top prize has helped world number four Trump to close the gap on third placed John Higgins in the rankings. It’s the eighth ranking crown of Trump’s career and sees him move ahead of Shaun Murphy to 12th in the list of most titles won. Bingham will rue relinquishing his 4-2 advantage this afternoon, when the ended the first session level at 4-4. However, the 2015 World Champion can reflect on his best tournament showing of the season and the £35,000 second prize. The evening session was played at thunderous pace, with both players going blow to blow with big breaks. Bingham was first to fire in a sublime century run of 130, before Trump restored parity at 5-5 with a contribution of 107. The Ace in the Pack claimed the following two frames to gain a two frame cushion at the mid-session, leading 7-5. However, Bingham dug deep to win consecutive frames and level once more at 7-7. From there Trump powered to the line, winning the fifteenth frame before signing off in style with a run of 107. Afterwards a delighted Trump immediately set his sights on further glory at next week’s English Open in Barnsley. The eight-time ranking event winner said: “It would be nice to win two tournaments in a row, that is another aim of mine. I want to do as well as possible in every tournament and try to win as many big events as I can. “I think it took me a few games to get used to being back out there, as I haven’t played much season. I wasn’t quite match sharp the first few games and I grew into the tournament. I played very well in the semi-final and that set me up for today. Stuart played great and maybe even better than me. I managed to stick in there and get to the interval at 4-4. Bingham was disappointed not to lift his second ranking silverware of the calendar year and felt he was playing well enough to be in the frame for the title. “I came out today and had three century breaks. I felt really comfortable,” said the 2015 World Champion.”There were about four frames that could have gone either way. He just had the run at the right time. I snookered him a couple of times and he got the snooker back. In the end it has finished 9-7, but I have to try to take the positives.” Article & Images courtesy of the World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Trump in a league of his own', 'trump-in-a-league-of-his-own', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Judd Trump won his third Championship League Snooker title as he came from behind to beat Ronnie O’Sullivan 3-2 in an exciting conclusion to the tournament at Crondon Park in Essex.', null, '“Trump’s win, his first title of the season, bagged him a total tournament prize of £20,400 as well as a spot in the exclusive big money Champion of Champions event which takes place in November.”', 'Judd Trump won his third Championship League Snooker title as he came from behind to beat Ronnie O’Sullivan 3-2 in an exciting conclusion to the tournament at Crondon Park in Essex.

more Trump previous won the title in 2014 and 2009 and proved the better man over the final two days as he beat Mark Williams in the semi-final and then O’Sullivan to take the glory. O’Sullivan had looked in unstoppable form this year with wins at the Masters and Welsh Open and had won 24 consecutive matches in 2016 before losing his final group game against Williams earlier in the day. The Bristolian won the opening frame of the final with a break of 86 before O’Sullivan hit back with 134 and 119 to lead 2-1. But Trump won a scrappy fourth frame before securing victory in the decider with a run of 73. “Championship League really suits my game; it is a tournament where I can relax and if you lose in the group stage you have other games,” said world number five Trump, who heads to Llandudno next week to defend his Ladbrokes World Grand Prix title. Trump’s win, his first title of the season, bagged him a total tournament prize of £20,400 as well as a spot in the exclusive big money Champion of Champions event which takes place in November. “I need to take that mentality into other events and I will be a lot better player. It seemed to be slipping away at one point when I missed a few balls but it was a good break in the last frame. “I didn’t play particularly great over the last few days but it is pleasing to win against some of the top players in the world. It gives me a lot of confidence ahead of the World Grand Prix where hopefully I can defend my title. “You need a bit of luck to win tournaments and I haven’t quite had that. I hadn’t won a tournament yet this season so it is a big one for me to get into the Champion of Champions.” Semi Finals Ronnie O’Sullivan 3-0 Matt Selt 76-22 (76), 86-0 (45, 41), 66-61 (O’Sullivan 34, Selt 51) Mark Williams 2-3 Judd Trump 25-60 (35), 112-3 (108), 4-102 (102), 89-0 (89), 0-92 (92) Final Ronnie O’Sullivan 2-3 Judd Trump 0-92 (86), 134-0 (134), 119-0 (119), 35-61, 0-103 (73, 30) Article & Images courtesy of the World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Trump leads the way', 'trump-leads-the-way', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Judd Trump continues to show he is snooker''s brightest young star.', null, '"Trump has been accused in the past of being a one-trick pony but here he has shown a completeness that will help him retain his number one status for the long haul.".', 'Judd Trump continues to show he is snooker''s brightest young star.

more His 10-8 victory against Neil Robertson clinched him the first International Championship title and proved in a week when the focus was all about the future - that he is leading the way. Judd is bold and he is brave on the table. He has instant star qualities but you can add ability to perform under pressure to his growing list of attributes. When the Aussie led the final 8-6, you feared he was about to be outfoxed by the classy all-rounder and final specialist. Robertson had lost just one competitive final before today. But when it mattered most, Trump found an extra gear and reeled off four straight frames to secure the £125,000 top prize and another trophy for his cabinet. Judd has had largely his own way this week. Fergal O''Brien and Aditya Mehta rolled over easily. Mark Allen came out all-guns blazing, which suits him a treat and Peter Ebdon failed to serve up the resilience predicted. Robertson managed to ask Judd some difficult questions, but again he had the answers. He stuck by his game, went for his pots and, importantly, played some of his best safety. Trump has been accused in the past of being a one-trick pony but here he has shown a completeness that will help him retain his number one status for the long haul. Some people suggest Trump must show his opponents more respect, but this would be losing his approach to the game that makes him so special. Judd plays the game with a youthful fearlessness. It''s an absolute joy to watch because he has the ability to pull it off. I hope he never changes because he''s box office at its best. Well played Judd. Article courtesy of the OneCue Images Courtesy of: Tai Chengzhe', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Trump takes CLS title No.2', 'trump-takes-cls-title-no-2', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'JUDD TRUMP completed a 3-1 victory over Martin Gould to win his second Championship League Snooker title and book himself a place in this year’s Champion of Champions event.', null, '"The Championship League has been good to me and I enjoy coming here. I go to China now and I want to carry this momentum forward and make sure I finish the season strongly."', 'JUDD TRUMP completed a 3-1 victory over Martin Gould to win his second Championship League Snooker title and book himself a place in this year’s Champion of Champions event.

more The Bristol potter looked at ease throughout the winners group, and takes away a total of £14,400 for his efforts in the internet streamed event. Trump was a winner of this internet streamed event in 2009, and played in Groups One and Two before qualifying via Group Three. His prize money this year takes his Championship League career earnings at a massive £90,467. “I always seem to play well here,” said a delighted Trump. “I was very lucky to get into the top four today and I needed to raise my game for the play-offs, and that is what happened. In the group game against Martin I had to play really well and that got me going in the final tonight. “The Championship League has been good to me and I enjoy coming here. I go to China now and I want to carry this momentum forward and make sure I finish the season strongly.” Trump went into a quick 2-0 lead in the best of five frame match and looked to be headed to victory but Gould pulled off a difficult 59 point clearance to steal the third frame by a single point and keep his hopes alive. A match-winning break of 109 though, after Gould missed the black off its spot, sealed the victory for Trump and he can head to China for the World Open with renewed confidence. Gould, who can console himself with a £9,200 pay cheque, was frustrated in defeat, “He played very well. I missed a couple of shots early doors and was punished severely. I didn’t get going in the final, especially considering how well I played in the semi-final. “I am disappointed but you can’t take anything away from Judd. He was the best player in the group. I was glad to get a frame because I wasn’t whitewashed in the tournament and it was good to keep that record.” Earlier, Gould had ended Stephen Maguire’s hopes and the Scotsman departed the competition £13,600 to the good. Trump eliminated Shaun Murphy in a final frame decider to leave the Group Five winner £22,500 in the black. The Championship League Snooker event is a venture between Matchroom Sport and Perform where 25 of the game''s finest players compete with all matches broadcast on a host of betting websites. Each group features seven players with each man playing the other six once in a round-robin basis. Every match is the best of five and the winner gains one point with the loser getting zero. At the end of the league phase the top four advances to that group''s semi-finals where the two winners meet in the final and the winner advances to the Winners'' Group at the end of March. The losing finalist, two losing semi-finalists and player finishing fifth move into the next group where they are joined by three new players and the process is repeated. Championship League Snooker will return in 2015. The Final - Judd Trump 3-1 Martin Gould 120-0 (Trump 119), 70-8 (Trump 36, 38), 64-65 (Trump 64, Gould 59), 109-1 (Trump 109) Article courtesy of the Championship League Snooker Images Courtesy of: Championship League Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Trump wins thrilling final', 'trump-wins-thrilling-final', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Judd Trump claimed his sixth ranking title in the most dramatic of finals, defeating Ronnie O’Sullivan to capture the European Masters title.', null, 'O’Sullivan remarked: “He deserved his victory and played the better snooker all day really. He’s too good a player to not be winning tournaments. You just have to hold your hands up and say well done. I didn’t do a lot wrong really. From 5-3 up to 6-5 down I had a couple of chances, but he just dominated the table.', 'Judd Trump claimed his sixth ranking title in the most dramatic of finals, defeating Ronnie O’Sullivan to capture the European Masters title.

more It was the pair’s sixth meeting in a professional final and Trump trailed for much of the match, before snatching the crown in the final frame and securing a 9-8 victory. Both players produced dominant performances yesterday, in a star studded semi-final line up. Trump defeated World Champion Mark Selby 6-2. O’Sullivan put on a snooker masterclass, as he demolished Neil Robertson 6-0 in just under an hour and a quarter. In the final it was O’Sullivan who was first to trouble the scorers. Trump made a break of 34 at the start of the frame, however he relinquished control of the table and the Rocket stole it on the black. The five-time World Champion O’Sullivan made breaks of 53 and 62, as he moved into a 3-1 lead. Trump hit back by producing runs of 120 and 82 to take two of the next three. O’Sullivan won the final frame of the session and held a 5-3 lead going into the evening. The 2011 UK Champion Trump immediately eliminated the deficit in the final session. A superb century break of 105 saw him draw level and a contribution of 67 in the eleventh frame put him ahead for the first time at 6-5. However, the Rocket turned on the after burners and cruised to the verge of victory. Runs of 93, 72 and 42 saw him win three on the bounce and go 8-6 up. Trump just wasn’t going away and he forced a decider, making another century of 109 in the process. In the final frame the Juddernaught produced a gutsy run of 74 to clinch the trophy. “I’m a little bit in disbelief,” said Trump. “To go 8-6 down was a little bit disheartening. I was faced with a difficult blue in the next frame and I just thought that I have to go for it. After that, I didn’t miss in the next three frames. To produce that under pressure against Ronnie will stand me in good stead for the rest of the season. “It’s nice to win this tournament and get my confidence back. I’m using a new cue and probably should have beaten Michael White in China. I was a little bit disheartened. I said to my manager that I want to change my cue again, but he told me to stick with it. Luckily I did.” It was only O’Sullivan’s second defeat in a ranking final since 2010 and the Rocket was in reflective mood afterwards. O’Sullivan remarked: “He deserved his victory and played the better snooker all day really. He’s too good a player to not be winning tournaments. You just have to hold your hands up and say well done. I didn’t do a lot wrong really. From 5-3 up to 6-5 down I had a couple of chances, but he just dominated the table. “I’ve battled it out, I played five or six matches this week and that’s exactly what I needed.” Article & Images courtesy of the World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Trump''s first professional 147', 'trumps-first-professional-147', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Judd Trump made the first maximum break of his career with a brilliant 147 at the Antwerp Open in Belgium, but lost the match 4-3 to Mark Selby.', null, '"It''s the 99th official maximum in snooker history and the first since Neil Robertson''s at the Wuxi Classic qualifiers last May. The 100th 147 can''t be far away and it will be a great milestone for snooker.""', 'Judd Trump made the first maximum break of his career with a brilliant 147 at the Antwerp Open in Belgium, but lost the match 4-3 to Mark Selby.

more Trump is a prolific break-builder who made 61 centuries last season so it''s perhaps surprising that it has taken him eight years as a professional, but he is now a member of the elite 147 club. The Bristol left-hander''s break came in the third frame of his last 32 match against Mark Selby at the Lotto Arena, when he was 2-0 down. He potted 15 reds with blacks and cleared the colours to the delight of the Belgian crowd. Trump''s break is worth £3,500 from the rolling prize pot for European Tour events, providing it is not equalled over the weekend. It''s the 99th official maximum in snooker history and the first since Neil Robertson''s at the Wuxi Classic qualifiers last May. The 100th 147 can''t be far away and it will be a great milestone for snooker. Selby won the fourth frame to lead 3-1 then Trump battled back to 3-3. He was on for another 147 in frame six but, with the balls in awkward positions, missed a tough penultimate red on 104. And it was UK and Masters champion Selby who had the last laugh with a break of 80 to win the deciding frame, earning a place in the last 16 on Sunday. Article courtesy of the World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Turn-up for the books as Treggy ''B'' team beat their ''A'' side', 'turn-up-for-the-books-as-treggy-b-team-beat-their-a-side', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘B’ recorded their first win of the season by beating their ‘A’ team 3-2, who stay top despite the defeat.', null, 'Mark Sturtridge put Bugle ‘B’ ahead at Mevagissey ‘B’ defeating Matt Chesterfield. The home side then reeled of three on the trot through Ben Rothwell, Matt Rowe and Nathan Shaw with Dave Rundle knocking in a majestic 35 break to get the second away point.', 'Tregonissey ‘B’ recorded their first win of the season by beating their ‘A’ team 3-2, who stay top despite the defeat.

more Barrie McIntosh and Jason Simmons got the leaders their points but great victories for Justin Hooley (Ryan Bilson), Simon Garrington (Martin Knight) and Andrew Gooch (Jason Cocks) sealed the memorable win. Leighton Varcoe and Paul Williams put Mevagissey ‘A’ 2-0 up at Bugle ‘A’. Gary Spencer and Jake Moore levelled things up leaving Paul Stutridge and James Popplewell to contest a tight final frame which Paul pinched to keep the Runabout Shield. Bethel ‘A’ came away with a great 4-1 win over Mevagissey ‘D’ – a result which could of easily been reversed given the closeness of the frames. Mark Ashton got the home point with Andy Peers, Matt Green, Garry Stephens and Leon ‘The Hatman’ Collard victorious for the visitors. Pelynt picked up a surprise 4-1 win at St Blazey to give them a great boost at the wrong end of the table. Len Flower and Dan Chivers put them 2-0 up only for Chris Morford to defeat the dangerous Phil Ormond but further wins for Richard Broad and Andy Parnell secured the win. Mark Sturtridge put Bugle ‘B’ ahead at Mevagissey ‘B’ defeating Matt Chesterfield. The home side then reeled of three on the trot through Ben Rothwell, Matt Rowe and Nathan Shaw with Dave Rundle knocking in a majestic 35 break to get the second away point. The top two in the Rees League locked horns and it was Mevagissey ‘C’ who came out on top 3-2 against Bethel ‘C’ with four frames decided on the black ball. Roger Smithson and Courtney Crockford took the home points with Joe and Matt Chesterfield along with Adrian Evans sealing the away win. St Stephen took all three points at home to Lostwithiel to jump right up to third place and look a real threat to the top two. Brown, Garside, Harrison, Bowyer and Parminter their winners with many twist and turns expected at the top of the table in the coming weeks. Gorran came away with a welcome win from Bugle ‘C’ – Luke Kennedy and Roy Bayliss took the home points with Lee McKinstry, Geoff Hayhurst and Steve Williams’ black-ball win over Nigel Couchman deciding the contest. Gavin Dyche knocked in the highest break in the league this year. His 38 helped Bethel ‘D’ get two points against Bethel ‘B’. Gavin’s nephew Dayle earned their team’s second point with Richie Bolt, Clive Stuthridge and Jim Tamblyn’s black ball win over Dave Morshead sealing the ‘B’ team’s win.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bugle ‘A’ 3 (G Spencer, J Moore, P Sturtridge) Mevagissey ‘A’ 2 (L Varcoe, P Williams), Tregonissey ‘A’ 2 (B McIntosh, J Simmons) Tregonissey ‘B’ 3 (J Hooley, S Garrington, A Gooch), St Blazey 1 (C Morford) Pelynt 4 (L Flower, D Chivers, R Broad, A Parnell), Mevagissey ‘B’ 3 (B Rothwell, M Rowe, N Shaw) Bugle ‘B’ 2 (M Sturtridge, D Rundle (35)), Mevagissey ‘D’ 1 (M Ashton) Bethel ‘A’ 4 (A Peers, M Green, G Stephens, L Collard). Rees Memorial League: Bugle ‘C’ 2 (L Kennedy, R Bayliss) Gorran 3 (L McKinstry, G Hayhurst, S Williams), Bethel ‘C’ 2 (R Smithson, C Crockford) Mevagissey ‘C’ 3 (J Chesterfield, M Chesterfield, A Evans), Bethel ‘D’ 2 (D Dyche, G Dyche (38)) Bethel ‘B’ 3 (R Bolt, C Stuthridge, J Tamblyn), St Stephen 5 (C Brown, R Garside, A Harrison, A Bowyer, D Parminter) Lostwithiel 0. Top breaks of the week: D Rundle 35, G Dyche 38

DRAWS FOR KNOCKOUT COMPETITIONS

St Blazey and District Snooker League have announced their competition draws, which were made by Stacey Hancock, Keith Moore and Mark Gregory. Seniors: Chris Perring (St Neot) v Phil Ormond (Pelynt), Len Flower (Pelynt) v John Daniell (Pelynt). Bill Toms: Andrew Best (Bugle) v Garry Stephens (Bethel), Jason Cocks (Tregonissey) v James Popplwell (Mevagissey) Barrie McIntosh (Tregonissey) v Mark Ashton (Mevagissey), Darren Lock (St Neot) v Steve Courts (St Neot). Doubles: S Courts and D Lock (St Neot) v A Maddams and M Ashton (St Neot and Mevagissey), G Dyche and D Dyche (Bethel) v J Popplewell and P Staley (Mevagissey), G Spencer and A Best (Bugle) v B McIntosh and R Bilson (Tregonissey), P Ormond and L Flower (Pelynt) v C Stuthridge/M Green (Bethel). Willie Thomas: Clive Stuthridge (Bethel) v Graham Hoskin (Lerryn), Terry Middleton (Lerryn) v Craig Nicholls (Lostwithiel), Matt Chesterfield (Mevagissey) v Roger Smithson (Bethel), Darren Biggen (Lostwithiel) v Carol Stephens (Tregonissey). Singles: Andy Peers (Bethel) v Gary Spencer (Bugle), Jason Cocks (Tregonissey) v Steve Courts (St Neot), J Pascoe or K Moore v Barrie McIntosh (Tregonissey), Matt Green (Bethel) v Mark Ashton (Mevagissey). Team Pairs: St Neot v Mevagissey ‘A’, Tregonissey ‘A’ v Mevagissey ‘B’. Home team/player to contact by January 28 and matches to be played by February 12. Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Two points dropped as Treggy fail to close the gap', 'two-points-dropped-as-treggy-fail-to-close-the-gap', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ missed a great chance to close in on the top of the table when dropping two point at Bugle ‘B’.', null, 'St Blazey ‘A’ got a good 3-2 away win at Pelynt. Liam Reynolds first game of the season ended in defeat to Phil Ormand and Pete Bate was beaten by John Daniell but wins for Leigh Rosevear, Stewart Dewings and Mickey Meares got the visitors home.', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ missed a great chance to close in on the top of the table when dropping two point at Bugle ‘B’.

more There were two terrific performances on the night from the home team with Dave Rundle hammering Jason Simmons and Colin Rundle’s excellent victory over Barrie McIntosh. Treggy responded with wins for Jason Cocks, Ryan Bilson and Chris Stephenson, who knocked in a 30. Mevagissey ‘A’ won’t feel so bad after losing at St Blazey ‘B’ by the odd frame. Paul Baron and Mike Hancock put the home side two up after defeating Matt Mills and Pete Chambers. Jamie Read knocked in a 32 but was well beaten by Paul Williams and then Chris Kendall levelled things up by beating Andrew Gooch only for Graham Taylor to see off Phil Staley to get the win which leaves the Blazey boys three points behind Meva but having played a game more with a game at Treggy up next. Bugle ‘A’ remain in fourth spot after a 4-1 victory at doomed Bethel. They raced into a 4-0 lead thanks to Paul Sturtridge, Ian Ball, Gary Spencer and Andrew Best, who netted a fine 43 break but Courtney Crockford hung on to Ian Powell’s shirt all night and potted most colours to get a respotted black which he potted superbly to register the home point. Bethel ‘D’ also stay well adrift of safety after losing to their ‘A’ side 3-2. Steve Benallack got them off to a good start seeing off Gary Stephens, Andy Peers and Geoff Hawken responded leaving Andy Spearman to draw things up after beating Leon Collard but Matt Green got the ‘A’ team’s third point beating Lee Searle. St Blazey ‘A’ got a good 3-2 away win at Pelynt. Liam Reynolds first game of the season ended in defeat to Phil Ormand and Pete Bate was beaten by John Daniell but wins for Leigh Rosevear, Stewart Dewings and Mickey Meares got the visitors home. Mevagissey ‘B’ only need a maximum of four points to win the league from their remaining three games after a 4-1 home victory over Gorran Haven. Paul Grimmett lost out on the black to Matt Rowe and Richard Holmes beat Dave Kendall in the last frame of the evening for Gorran’s point with Mark Gregory, Ben Rothwell and Nathan Shaw got the champions elect’s points. Bethel ‘B’ remain third after a 3-2 way at St Stephen ‘B’ Craig Nicholls and a walkover for Terry Nicholls secured the home points with Guy Buckle, Clive Stuthridge and Jim Tamblyn recorded the win for the away side. Mevagissey ‘C’ and St Stephen ‘A’ fought out a very entertaining night. The best game of the match was between James Prynn and Stuart Bell. Superb safety and potting ending in a respoted black with Stuart prevailing. Ben Prynn, Matt and Joe Chesterfield got the home points with John Bennett beating the predator for St Stephen’s second point. Breaks of 31 by George Lory and a 30 by Rich Lakeman along with a win for Ian Latham helped Mevagissey ‘D’ come away from Treggy ‘B’ with a 3-2 win which keeps them in with an outside chance of promotion. On target for the home side were Phil Hooley and Gary Wherry.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bethel ‘C’ 1 (Courtney Crockford) Bugle ‘A’ 4 (Paul Sturtridge, Ian Ball, Gary Spencer, Andrew Best (43)), Pelynt 2 (Phil Ormand, John Daniell) St Blazey ‘A’ 3 (Leigh Rosevear, Steve Dewings, Mikey Meares), Bugle ‘B’ 2 (Dave Rundle, Colin Rundle) Tregonissey ‘A’ 3 (Jason Cocks, Ryan Bilson, Chris Stephenson (30)), St Blazey ‘B’ 3 (Paul Baron, Mike Hancock, Graham Taylor) Mevagissey ‘A’ 2 (Paul Williams, Chris Kendall), Bethel ‘D’ 2 (Andy Spearman, Steve Benallack) Bethel ‘A’ 3 (Andy Peers, Geoff Hawken, Matt Green). Rees Memorial League: Mevagissey ‘C’ 3 (Ben Prynn, Matt Chesterfield, Joe Chesterfield) St Stephen ‘A’ 2 (Stuart Bell, John Bennettt), St Stephen ‘B’ 2 (Craig Nicholls, Terry Nicholls) Bethel ‘B’ 3 (Guy Buckle, Clive Stuthridge, Jim Tamblyn), Tregonissey ‘A’ 2 (Philip Hooley, Gary Wherry) Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (George Lory (31), Ian Latham, Rich Lakeman (30)) Mevagissey ‘B’ 4 (Mark Gregory, Ben Rothwell, Matt Rowe, Nathan Shaw) Gorran Haven 1 (Richard Holmes). Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: A Best 43, C Stephenson 30, J Read 32, G Lory 31, R Lakeman 30 Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Unexpected results in final week of the season', 'unexpected-results-in-final-week-of-the-season', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'The final week of the league season saw some unexpected results, none more so than bottom of the table Lostwithiel B, who after being 2-0 down to third place Tregonissey sneaked a win courtesy of Bryan Warne, John…', null, '"Steve Bampton’s victory over James Tomlinson was the only bright spark for the Bodmin boys as Scott Mills, Rob Blamey, Richard Lakeman and Neil Parker secured a fifth place finish for the D team who will surely be targeting a promotion push next season. "', 'The final week of the league season saw some unexpected results, none more so than bottom of the table Lostwithiel B, who after being 2-0 down to third place Tregonissey sneaked a win courtesy of Bryan Warne, John Daniell and Lenny Flowers.

more The result was enough to move them above Bethel D, who crashed 4-1 at St Blazey B. Mike Hancock’s two breaks of 39 saw him win the victory league player of the year by only two points from Barrie McIntosh. Graham Taylor’s 30 and Paul Baron’s 32 followed a fine 35 break by Steve ‘Pointblank’ Benallack, which saw off Andrew Gooch. Bethel B got the better of St Blazey A to secure fourth place with four frames going to the black, and Gary Stephens using up all his luck to see off the unlucky Leigh Rosevear. Lostwithiel A finished well by winning 4-1 against St Neot with Anthony Maddam’s coolness personified when beating Neil Collins. Bugle B continued their good run defeating Mevagissey A 4-1 as Mark Sturtridge’s 36 against Leighton Varcoe pivotal. Matt Mills defeat of Dave Rundle was the seasider’s only bright spot. Mevagissey B captured the Rees League title after defeating Bugle C 4-1, they will be much welcomed in the top league next season. Nathan Shaw secured the title by winning the first frame followed by wins by Mark Gregory, Dave Kendall and Ben Rothwell, with Jake Conroy replying. Meva’s C and D teams both recorded final day 4-1 wins with the C team getting an excellent victory over St Stephens. Ben Prynn’s thrashing of Gary Clark was the standout frame of the evening. Steve Bampton’s victory over James Tomlinson was the only bright spark for the Bodmin boys as Scott Mills, Rob Blamey, Richard Lakeman and Neil Parker secured a fifth place finish for the D team who will surely be targeting a promotion push next season. Gorran Haven are probably sad to see the season end as they recorded a second 4-1 victory on the trot as team members Grimmett, Reddall, Bunney and McKinstry saw them return from Lerryn victorious. Top breaks of the week: M Hancock 39, 39, G Taylor 30, P Baron 32 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Victory For Allen In Germany', 'victory-for-allen-in-germany', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Mark Allen won his first title of the season by beating Ding Junhui 4-1 in the final of the Ruhr Open in Germany.', null, '"The way my form has been over the last few months, it''s a big achievement to win here," said Allen. "A lot of it is down to Terry Griffiths, we did some work last week and he made a change on the technical side and helped me to be more positive. I need to keep putting the work in with him and hopefully it will pay dividends."', 'Mark Allen won his first title of the season by beating Ding Junhui 4-1 in the final of the Ruhr Open in Germany.

more Northern Ireland''s 27-year-old Allen outplayed China''s Ding in the final of the season''s fifth European Tour event to take the 25,000 Euro top prize. Left-hander Allen won seven matches during the event to land his first title since he won the Haikou World Open seven months ago. He took the opening frame of the final with a break of 76. Ding looked set to level until he missed a red to a centre pocket on 55, and his opponent battled his way back into the frame and took it on the colours. A superb run of 100 put Allen 3-0 ahead, before Ding pulled one back with runs of 40 and 32. But there was to be no fight back as Allen''s 74 in frame five saw him over the winning line. "The way my form has been over the last few months, it''s a big achievement to win here," said Allen. "A lot of it is down to Terry Griffiths, we did some work last week and he made a change on the technical side and helped me to be more positive. I need to keep putting the work in with him and hopefully it will pay dividends. "I was able to have a couple of hours rest after my semi-final whereas Ding had a long match, so maybe he was not as fresh as me for the final, though I''m sure he was just as motivated. "The crowd and the atmosphere here were brilliant. Snooker is taking off in mainland Europe which is good to see." The tournament brought snooker to the town of Mulheim for the first time, and drew large crowds throughout the three days, emphasising snooker''s popularity in Germany. World number 75 Robbie Williams, from The Wirral, was the event''s surprise package as he reached the semi-finals before losing 4-0 to Allen. Ding edged out Stephen Maguire 4-3. The European Tour comprises eight events, with the top 24 on the final Order of Merit to qualify for the Grand Finals next March, to be joined by eight more players from the Asian Tour Order of Merit. The next event is the Kay Suzanne Memorial Cup in Gloucester from November 7 to 10. Results (last 16 onwards) Last 16 Ben Woollaston 4-0 Chris Norbury Mark King 3-4 Stephen Maguire Barry Hawkins 2-4 Ryan Day Ding Junhui 4-3 Anthony Hamilton David Gilbert 3-4 Robbie Williams Luca Brecel 4-1 Stuart Bingham Kyren Wilson 2-4 Judd Trump Mark Davis 3-4 Mark Allen Quarter-finals Woollaston 3-4 Maguire Day 1-4 Ding Williams 4-3 Brecel Trump 1-4 Allen Semi-finals Maguire 3-4 Ding Williams 0-4 Allen Final Ding 1-4 Allen Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: Monique Limbos', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('When Gary met Barrie', 'when-gary-met-barrie', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Bethel ‘B’ came from 2-0 down to beat their ‘D’ team to move up to second in the table.', 'St Blazey dropped their first point of the season in the Rees League as Courtney Crockford beat Liam Reynolds on a respot to give Bethel ‘C’ their only point – Chris Brown’s 36 break seeing off the dangerous Jason Cocks.', 'Mevagissey ‘B’ recorded an excellent victory against their ‘A’ side who they defeated 4-1. Only the in-form Matt Fox saving a whitewash.', 'Bethel ‘B’ came from 2-0 down to beat their ‘D’ team to move up to second in the table.

Mevagissey ‘B’ recorded an excellent victory against their ‘A’ side who they defeated 4-1. Only the in-form Matt Fox saving a whitewash.

Gary Spencer’s fine 60 break helped Bugle ‘A’ move up to joint top spot as they beat Lostwithiel ‘B’ 4-1.

Gary Stephens heralded his return to playing by beating Barry McIntosh, but was Bethel’s only point as Tregonissey closed in on the top two.

